globalThis.OPENFSX_DOCS = {
  "FLIGHT": {
    "source": "docs/FLIGHT.md",
    "title": "Flight prototype",
    "html": "<h1>Flight prototype</h1>\n<p>openFSX now has an initial keyboard-controlled airborne mode for the local stock C172. It loads the aircraft&#39;s CFG and legacy AIR data, simulates six degrees of freedom, and renders the installed MDLX/DDS content independently of fsx.exe.</p>\n<p>This is an experimental flight-dynamics subset with an optional fixed-wheel runway start. Ground roll and departure are demonstrated; FSX behavior parity, a complete aircraft system model and validated landing behavior are absent.</p>\n<p>The visual model now follows the physical center-wheel steering angle through its own animation binding. Q/E therefore changes both contact-force direction and the nose-wheel pose. Supported XAPS/XAPC targets can use model Lag limits on the simulation clock; pause holds the pose and reset initializes it again. The current visual adapter also drives the C172 throttle expression using instance-local numeric data and scratch registers, and exposes the on-ground state. A local interior-model check verified throttle movement. Cockpit flight selection now reads the native interior reference and configured eye point; gauges and panel interaction remain unimplemented. It does not invent RPM or control-surface percent mappings. C172 steering was checked from the shared keyboard controller through physics to offscreen GPU output; original-FSX motion and timing comparisons remain unverified. See the <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">viewer guide</a> for the reproducible local-content check.</p>\n<h2>Start a flight</h2>\n<p>From the repository root, with your own FSX installation under FSX:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --fly --variant 0 --windows-1252 --lod 150</code></pre>\n<p>Add --cockpit to start in the installed interior model, or press F9 during flight. F11 returns to the exterior. Model selection and eye position follow the selected variant and its CFG datum; dynamic gauge surfaces remain blank.</p>\n<p>In flight mode the positional path is an aircraft folder, not an MDL file. The selected fltsim.N entry resolves the AIR file, exterior model.cfg entry and texture folder. Variant 0 selects the Blue/Gold C172 in the verified installation. Encoding selection is explicit; omit --windows-1252 for UTF-8 CFG files.</p>\n<p>The flight starts airborne at 90 knots true airspeed and 300 metres above a local flat plane. Initial throttle and elevator trim are solved for level flight once. There is no autopilot keeping it level after pilot input.</p>\n<p>Add --ground for a runway start:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --fly --ground --windows-1252 --lod 150</code></pre>\n<p>This settles the suspension with power off, positions the aircraft near the south end of the original test runway, and applies the parking brake. B releases the brake; F4 selects full power and F1 idle. Rudder also steers the nosewheel. Use pitch input and trim to control departure; sustained full power with the initial trim can produce a large nose-up transient and loss of speed. Initial runway trim comes from a 65 kt / 300 m level-trim solve; it is not a C172 POH takeoff-trim specification. R resets to the selected ground or airborne start.</p>\n<h2>Controls</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Key</th><th scope=\"col\">Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Down / Up</td><td>Pull / push elevator</td></tr><tr><td>Left / Right</td><td>Bank left / right</td></tr><tr><td>Q / E</td><td>Rudder left / right</td></tr><tr><td>Page Up / Page Down</td><td>Increase / decrease throttle</td></tr><tr><td>F1 / F4</td><td>Idle / full throttle</td></tr><tr><td>F9 / F11</td><td>Virtual cockpit / exterior chase; F9 also recenters cockpit look</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Toggle parking brake</td></tr><tr><td>Space</td><td>Hold wheel brakes</td></tr><tr><td>Home / End</td><td>Nose-up / nose-down elevator trim</td></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>Pause / resume</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>Reset the selected ground or airborne start</td></tr><tr><td>Left drag in cockpit</td><td>Look around relative to the aircraft</td></tr><tr><td>Mouse wheel</td><td>Chase distance or cockpit vertical field of view</td></tr><tr><td>Esc</td><td>Close window</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Releasing a directional key centers that control. Throttle and trim remain at their last positions. Opposing keys cancel each other. Keyboard deflections use half the configured control range. Losing window focus or minimizing pauses the flight and clears held controls; press P to resume.</p>\n<p>A tap completed between physics steps is sampled for one 1/120-second step, then released. Dedicated navigation keys and NumLock-off numpad navigation events share the same controls. Number input with NumLock on is not treated as a directional command. Letter bindings use physical key positions.</p>\n<p>The window title reports live flight state. Add <strong>--debug-hud</strong> for the optional original telemetry overlay; it is hidden by default. The camera follows the aircraft. The runway and grid are original test geometry, not FSX scenery. Wheel contact continues the rigid-body simulation, including compression, braking and liftoff. Scrape points, damage and validated landing handling are not implemented. CG penetration or an invalid numeric state terminates the run with a diagnostic.</p>\n<h2>On-screen flight panel</h2>\n<ul><li>TAS is true airspeed in knots, not indicated airspeed.</li><li>ALT is height in feet above the flat test plane, not pressure altitude.</li><li>V/S is vertical speed in feet per minute, positive upwards.</li><li>HDG is heading in degrees clockwise from the local test world&#39;s north. No magnetic model or geographic navigation is implemented.</li><li>THR and TRIM are normalized throttle and elevator-trim commands in percent.</li><li>BRK reports the wheel-brake command in percent; it includes parking brake or held Space.</li><li>The attitude display uses body roll and pitch in degrees. Pitch marks are spaced by 10 degrees; right bank rotates the horizon counterclockwise, and pitch-up moves it below the fixed aircraft symbol.</li><li>AIRBORNE, PAUSED, POST-PEAK LIFT and ON WHEELS status come from the simulation. The post-peak message is a curve-region diagnostic, not a validated stall warning system.</li></ul>\n<p>The panel scales with the window and is rendered by the same GPU path in offscreen captures. Its original geometry and pixel letterforms require no external fonts, gauge files or textures. It is a diagnostic flight overlay; FSX cockpit instruments and XML gauges remain unimplemented. The panel updates per rendered frame; the window title updates four times per second.</p>\n<h2>Reproducible checks</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run -- inspect-aircraft &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --windows-1252\ncargo run -- flight-check &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --windows-1252\ncargo run -- takeoff-check &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --windows-1252\ncargo test --workspace --all-features --locked</code></pre>\n<p>flight-check loads variant 0, reports converted parameters, then runs fixed-control trim, initial control-sign, full-power response and power-off pull scenarios. Its acceptance gates check internal numerical consistency and signs. They are not validation against recorded FSX trajectories.</p>\n<p>An offscreen flight frame uses the same geometry and GPU pipeline as the window:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --fly --debug-hud --windows-1252 --lod 150 --seconds 10 --snapshot target/c172-flight.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>The snapshot advances the trimmed state at 120 Hz before rendering. Duration is limited to 300 seconds and requires --fly and --snapshot. Keep working captures under ignored target. Selected PNG engine screenshots are published in the website gallery at the project owner&#39;s request; source game resources are never published. Convert captures losslessly with <code>node scripts/ppm-to-png.mjs target/c172-flight.ppm target/c172-flight.png</code>.</p>\n<h2>Model and units</h2>\n<p>State uses metres, seconds, kilograms and radians. World axes are north/east/down; body axes are forward/right/down. Orientation is a normalized quaternion. A fourth-order Runge-Kutta integrator uses a 1/120-second outer step, with smaller contact steps near the ground. Desktop scheduling accumulates frame time and caps any one frame at 0.25 seconds; long stalls do not trigger unbounded catch-up.</p>\n<p>Rendering maps NED to east/up/north and uses a left-handed Direct3D projection, matching the native MDLX convention. The corrected camera preserves left/right and pilot-view bank direction; a former right-handed projection mirrored them. This visual correction does not change the physics integration or its control signs. Exterior and cockpit cameras share the same loaded-CG transform.</p>\n<p>CFG supplies maximum gross mass, empty mass and inertias, loading stations, tank positions and capacities, wing geometry, engine position/power, control limits, datum and tuning scalars. Initial loading uses the configured station weights and full usable Avgas at an explicit fixed 6 lb/US gallon. Unusable fuel is assumed included in the configured empty weight and is not added twice. This is a deterministic loading choice, not an import of the user&#39;s saved FSX flight or fuel state. Overweight loading and other fuel types are rejected.</p>\n<p>The mass-weighted CG includes passengers and fuel. Empty-aircraft inertia is translated to the loaded CG with the parallel-axis theorem; stations and tanks are point masses. The full symmetric 3-by-3 tensor includes their products of inertia. Cholesky factorization rejects non-positive tensors and solves the rigid-body Euler equation, including gyroscopic coupling. A nonzero configured empty_weight_coupled_moi remains unsupported; loading-induced coupling is supported. Distributed tank shape, fuel slosh and fuel consumption are absent.</p>\n<p>As an explicit initial approximation, AIR aerodynamic moments are treated as referenced to the empty CG. The aerodynamic wrench is translated to the loaded CG; propeller thrust acts at Engine.0. Initial pitch trim uses those same moment equations. Laterally unbalanced loading/engine placement is rejected by the initial level-trim solver, which does not yet solve aileron/rudder trim. CFG static height/pitch initialize ground settling; they do not constrain the aircraft once integration starts.</p>\n<h2>Fixed-wheel ground subset</h2>\n<p>Only three fixed wheels are supported. Wheel positions, static compression, travel ratio, damping ratio, brake mapping and steering angle are read from contact_points. Non-wheel contacts are omitted and counted; retracting gear, invalid compression/damping and other wheel counts are rejected. Impact damage and wheel-extension airspeed fields are not implemented. Contact locations are treated as uncompressed tire contact points, without adding wheel radius again.</p>\n<p>Empty-weight support fractions balance vertical load and roll/pitch moments over the wheel triangle. Spring stiffness comes from that support load and configured static compression; damping uses the configured ratio. Normal force cannot pull a tire onto the plane. Travel beyond the configured stroke uses an explicit stiffer stop. Tire forces use regularized friction: 0.02 rolling, up to 0.8 braking/lateral, with a combined friction limit. These coefficients and the stop are openFSX choices, not decoded FSX surface/material behavior. No wheel angular dynamics, anti-skid, distributed tire patch, scrape response, ground effect, runway slope or weather model is implemented.</p>\n<p>The outer control update remains 120 Hz; within 10 m of the test plane, RK4 subdivides integration to at most 1/480 second for contact stability. Runway initialization settles for five simulated seconds with idle power and brakes, then resets the public scenario clock. There is no hidden position constraint or controller during the subsequent run.</p>\n<p>takeoff-check verifies 20 seconds of power-off parking, braking from 10 m/s, and a departure sequence: full power, brake release, normalized pull 0.12 for two seconds when speed first reaches 55 kt, then neutral elevator. The gate requires height above 30 m within 60 seconds. The 55 kt trigger is a test input, not an established rotation-speed recommendation. On the local C172:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Check</th><th scope=\"col\">Observed result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Settled ground start</td><td>CG height 1.2116 m; pitch 1.655 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>20 s idle parking</td><td>North drift -0.0003 m; speed below displayed precision</td></tr><tr><td>Braking from 10 m/s</td><td>14.45 m; final speed 0.0005 m/s after 10 s</td></tr><tr><td>First airborne clearance above 3 m</td><td>18.65 s; 480.91 m north travel; 82.07 kt</td></tr><tr><td>Departure height gate</td><td>30.07 m after 22.02 s; 75.74 kt; transient climb 11.97 m/s</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Clearance above 3 m is not the instant of first wheel separation. These are internal scenario results, not validated C172 takeoff performance. The large transient climb and speed exchange require recorded reference comparison. In the native window, F4 and B changed the live power/brake readouts and the aircraft departed the runway; one observed frame showed about 81 kt and 32 ft with AIRBORNE status. P paused and R restored the braked runway start. A longer unchanged full-power run developed high pitch and loss of speed; sustained departure control and landing behavior remain unverified.</p>\n<p>AIR records 0x0404 and 0x0473 supply piecewise-linear lift and pitch-moment curves versus body angle of attack. Selected coefficients from the 250-byte 0x1101 record supply parasite/gear drag, control derivatives, damping, sideslip and cross-axis terms. Integer coefficients use their documented 1/2048 scale; double coefficients are not rescaled. Legacy pitch/yaw and control signs are converted to the right-handed body convention. Modern override records 0x1539 through 0x1547 are rejected rather than silently ignored.</p>\n<p>Forces include lift, drag, side force, propeller thrust and gravity. Moments include the loaded static/control/damping derivatives and rigid-body gyroscopic coupling. Induced drag uses the conventional lift-squared/wing-aspect-ratio relation. Density follows the ISA troposphere, limited to 0–11 km.</p>\n<p>The propeller is an explicit approximation: an ideal momentum-theory disk with a fixed effective efficiency of 0.8, sea-level rated shaft power, throttle and density scaling. It remains finite at zero airspeed. FSX engine/propeller lookup tables, mixture, RPM dynamics, propwash moments, torque and fuel consumption are not implemented. This is not a claim of an accurate C172 engine model.</p>\n<h2>Observed results</h2>\n<p>On 17 October 2026, with the local C172 at loaded mass <strong>1,046.89 kg</strong>: 748.43 kg empty, 154.22 kg configured payload, and 144.24 kg full usable fuel. Maximum gross mass is 1,156.66 kg. Body CG is [0.145084, 0, -0.104990] metres. These results supersede the earlier maximum-gross/empty-CG approximation.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Check</th><th scope=\"col\">Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Derived clean sea-level stall</td><td>50.97 kt; CFG reference 53.00 kt, with no loading condition established for that reference</td></tr><tr><td>90 kt / 300 m initial trim</td><td>Throttle 0.50830; normalized trim -0.19759; AoA 1.972 degrees</td></tr><tr><td>60 s fixed-control level flight</td><td>90.00000 kt; 300.00000 m; no controller corrections</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 s pull input 0.1</td><td>Positive pitch rate, 6.1515 degrees/s</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 s right-bank input 0.1</td><td>Positive roll rate, 3.3736 degrees/s</td></tr><tr><td>0.5 s right-rudder input 0.1</td><td>Positive yaw rate, 2.4879 degrees/s</td></tr><tr><td>30 s full power from 90 kt / 1500 m</td><td>87.16 kt; height gain 111.73 m; final climb 1.66 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>20 s power-off pull from 65 kt / 1500 m</td><td>Reached angles beyond the lift-curve peak</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The full-power values describe a transient scenario, not a steady-state climb specification. The post-peak flag establishes curve entry, not validated stall, spin or recovery behavior.</p>\n<p>The NVIDIA RTX 2080 / Vulkan path produced an offscreen flight frame and presented a frame in the native window. Automated tests exercise keyboard mapping, short taps, key release, pause/reset and frame partitioning.</p>\n<p>End-to-end window input was rechecked on 17 October 2026 with the loaded-mass model and on-screen panel using Windows Computer Use keyboard injection. A Right tap changed displayed bank from +0.0 to +0.1 degrees. A subsequent Down tap was followed by pitch +2.1 degrees and approximately +20 ft/min climb, compared with the initial +2.0 degrees and zero climb. These values are observations after short impulses, not controlled performance benchmarks. P displayed PAUSED on the panel; R restored 90.0 kt, 984 ft, zero bank and initial trim; Esc closed the process successfully. Earlier event tracing identified an injected arrow as physical Numpad6 and logical ArrowRight; both dedicated and numpad navigation are handled. This check establishes delivered window input, simulation response and visible telemetry changes. Sustained physical keyboard holds, controller hardware, other aircraft, graphics backends and platforms remain unverified.</p>\n<h2>Experimental native instruments</h2>\n<p>Add <code>--native-gauges --cockpit</code> on Windows after building the separate x86 helper with <code>scripts/build-gauge-host.ps1</code>. Five native C172 needles consume ideal ISA pitot/static air data from the existing simulation: calibrated airspeed, standard-setting pressure altitude and vertical speed. Three native attitude/heading sprites also consume ideal gyro orientation. Pause freezes callback evaluation; reset restarts the helper and clears native state. The local integration check covers flight-driven GPU pixels and lifecycle behavior. See <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">the viewer guide</a> for commands, assumptions and verification limits. Other instruments remain static or blank; gyro drift/failures and adjustment knobs are absent; FSX instrument accuracy, weather, pressure adjustment, response lag, panel interaction and engine RPM remain unimplemented or unverified.</p>\n<h2>Remaining work</h2>\n<p>Recorded reference flights and quantitative comparison, fuel consumption and configurable loading, engine/propeller tables, broader ground handling and landing validation, control-surface animation, cockpit instruments, audio and controller hardware. MDLX skinning/visibility and generated registration decals remain viewer limitations. No BGL terrain, geographic navigation or add-on API compatibility is implied by this local flight test.</p>\n<p>AIR field research was checked against the AirEd definitions linked from <a href=\"https://www.aero.sors.fr/fsairfile.html\">Herve Sors&#39; aircraft dynamics resources</a>. The reference archive was read locally and is excluded from Git. No third-party implementation or game resource is redistributed.</p>\n<p>Legacy loading-field meanings were checked against the <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/simobjects/aircraft_configuration_files.html\">Prepar3D SDK aircraft configuration reference</a>. Point-mass inertia, fixed fuel density and aerodynamic moment reference choices above are openFSX approximations, not claims about FSX&#39;s internal implementation.</p>"
  },
  "VIEWER": {
    "source": "docs/VIEWER.md",
    "title": "Model viewer",
    "html": "<h1>Model viewer</h1>\n<p>The native viewer reads FSX MDLX geometry and supported diffuse DDS/BMP textures directly. It is a development tool, not a simulator or a converter.</p>\n<p>The same executable also has an experimental --fly mode with an aircraft-folder argument. Its keyboard bindings and simulation behavior differ from orbit inspection: see the <a href=\"#/docs/FLIGHT\">flight guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>Build and run</h2>\n<p>Use stable Rust, the Windows MSVC toolchain and a GPU with current Vulkan or DirectX 12 drivers. wgpu chooses an available backend:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>For orbit inspection pass an explicit MDL path from your own installation. Flight mode resolves exterior model and texture paths through the selected aircraft.cfg/model.cfg variant.</p>\n<h2>Texture selection</h2>\n<p>Use --texture-dir to choose a livery directory. Without it the viewer looks for the aircraft&#39;s sibling texture folder. Use --no-textures for a material-color preview; it cannot be combined with --texture-dir.</p>\n<p>The selected directory takes precedence over its texture.cfg fallbacks, visited in numeric order. Case-insensitive ASCII file lookup handles MDLX uppercase names on case-sensitive filesystems. Cycles are ignored and search is limited to 64 directories. No file in the installation is modified.</p>\n<p>The decoder supports the top mip of ordinary 2D DXT1, DXT3, DXT5 and masked 32-bit RGB DDS, plus 4/8/24-bit uncompressed BITMAPINFOHEADER BMP files. DDS/BMP selection uses the file signature. Dimensions are limited to 4096, input files to 256 MiB, and the total selected diffuse textures to 256 MiB decoded. Cubemaps, volumes, DX10 formats, compressed/extended BMP variants and other compression variants are rejected with a diagnostic. Missing textures use material-color fallback. Generated registration decals are not available as ordinary DDS files and remain unsupported.</p>\n<h2>Static panel artwork inspection</h2>\n<p>This separate offscreen example decodes the stock C172 gauge-library bitmap resources and binds the static root images to the configured interior textures. It does not run native callbacks, draw needles/moving layers/digital values or change the default flight view&#39;s blank gauges. Use a new output directory and the exact resource language ID (1033 in the checked installation):</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-render --example panel-artwork-check -- &quot;./FSX&quot; target/panel-artwork-01 1033</code></pre>\n<p>The check decodes all 1,022 bitmap resources from the five native libraries used by the C172 VC, plus seven BMP files in its panel folder. It renders 28 static root images through the two 512x512 panel textures, with the XML registration plaque reported unsupported. Before/after 960x720 snapshots use the same camera, geometry and GPU device. This is unlit source-artwork composition followed by the viewer&#39;s approximate material lighting, not operational instruments or verified FSX panel rasterization. Raw panel atlases stay under ignored target.</p>\n<h2>Native needle result inspection</h2>\n<p>The separate native-needle-check example consumes the explicit output of the x86 update probe. It draws six original bitmap needles using native callback results, authored POS/OFS pivots and nonlinearity tables. The three selected altimeter callbacks are explicitly interpreted as degrees when no table exists; the example does not generalize that interpretation to other table-free gauges.</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-gauge-host.ps1\n./scripts/check-native-gauges.ps1 -FsxRoot ./FSX -Output target/needle-inputs.ndjson\ncargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example native-needle-check -- ./FSX target/needle-inspection target/needle-inputs.ndjson</code></pre>\n<p>Use new output paths. The check renders three 960x720 snapshots on one GPU device, with a fixed camera and 28 static root images. It preserves the authored next-list order for overlapping needles and reads exact language 1033 resources. Six bindings are included: airspeed, tachometer, vertical speed and three altimeter needles. The input states are explicit raw probe values, not a flight recording. Schema, context, module lifecycle, resource/token IDs, header versions, finite values and sample counts are checked before use.</p>\n<p>This path does not execute DLLs from Rust, run temporal needle lag, supply pressure/air-data/calibration behavior or integrate instruments into --fly. Other moving/static layers, digital values, masks and panel interaction remain omitted. Original-FSX visual/behavioral parity has not been measured.</p>\n<h2>Controls</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Input</th><th scope=\"col\">Action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Left mouse drag</td><td>Orbit around model bounds</td></tr><tr><td>Mouse wheel</td><td>Zoom within distance limits</td></tr><tr><td>Arrow keys</td><td>Rotate camera</td></tr><tr><td>R</td><td>Reset framing</td></tr><tr><td>Esc</td><td>Close viewer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rendering is event driven and does not redraw continuously while idle. Resizing rebuilds the depth buffer. Zero-size minimized windows are not configured as render surfaces.</p>\n<h2>LOD selection</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run -- inspect-model &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>The inspector prints each LOD&#39;s parts, triangle count, skipped skinned parts and transformed bounds. Use <code>--lod N</code> to choose a level; the default is the highest numeric LOD. Malformed data produces an error instead of unchecked indexing.</p>\n<h2>Offscreen validation</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --snapshot target/c172.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>The same depth-tested pipeline renders without a window and writes a binary PPM. Keep working snapshots under ignored <code>target/</code>. Selected PNG engine screenshots are published in the website gallery at the project owner&#39;s request; original game resource files remain excluded. Use <code>node scripts/ppm-to-png.mjs target/c172.ppm target/c172.png</code> for lossless conversion.</p>\n<h2>Known limits</h2>\n<p>Diffuse textures and flat face lighting only, with approximate ordinary alpha blending and MDLX alpha tests. Transparent parts are sorted by their centers; intersecting transparent triangles are not sorted individually. Other blend modes, accurate FSX material lighting, specular/bump/reflection maps and texture mip chains are absent. Visibility supports a bounded read-only numeric subset, described below. Automatic animation drivers, skinning, attachments and effects remain incomplete. Manual native-key inspection is described below. Unavailable visibility variables can expose normally hidden alternatives, including multiple propeller states. Startup diagnostics report these limits.</p>\n<p>Windows x64 is the initial target. The first offscreen C172 render was verified on an NVIDIA RTX 2080 through Vulkan. Building on another platform or backend does not establish rendering support there.</p>\n<h2>FSX-style menu on Windows</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX&quot; --menu</code></pre>\n<p>The menu host requires the Microsoft Edge WebView2 Runtime. It reads the local installation&#39;s <code>uires</code> bitmap skin and aircraft thumbnails; these assets are not included in openFSX. No original executable or gauge DLL is loaded. The independent HTML/CSS layout is not yet pixel-verified against FSX.</p>\n<p><strong>CHANGE...</strong> under Current Aircraft opens the installation catalog, with a manufacturer filter and variation selection. <strong>FLY NOW!</strong> starts the selected variant on the experimental test runway. Unsupported aircraft report an error; being listed does not establish flight support. <strong>Esc</strong> closes the flight window and restores the menu. Native C172 launch and return were checked on Windows.</p>\n<p>Location, weather, time/season, fuel/payload editing, flight plans, saved flights, failures and other sidebar sections remain unimplemented. The aircraft image is a live native MDLX/DDS preview. It slowly rotates; drag with the left mouse button to change the view and use the wheel to zoom. Selection reloads the exterior model and livery without requiring a supported AIR flight model. The renderer hides behind menu dialogs and while flight runs. A failed model load clears the previous aircraft and reports an error. Only windowed launch is supported. The menu catalog currently explicitly decodes CFG files as Windows-1252. Flight telemetry is hidden by default; use <strong>--debug-hud</strong> when launching flight directly to enable the independent debugging overlay.</p>\n<p>The host serves only its own HTML/catalog, an exact bitmap allowlist and indexed thumbnail paths canonicalized inside the installation. It does not serve the FSX directory as a general web root or expose filesystem paths in the catalog.</p>\n<h2>Inspect authored animation keys</h2>\n<p>List the model&#39;s animation catalog first:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -- inspect-model &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot;\ncargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --animation 7 --frame 50 --view rear --zoom 1.6</code></pre>\n<p>For the verified stock C172, animation 7 is the elevator group. Indexes are file-local, not a universal aircraft API. <strong>[</strong> and <strong>]</strong> scrub five authored keyframes at a time. The title displays the selected clip and frame. Rotation uses normalized XYZW quaternions and shortest-arc spherical interpolation; translation is linear. Native key deltas compose after the static local node matrix, then inherit parent transforms. Draw topology, texture coordinates and material selection stay fixed while positions, face normals and draw centers update. No original animation DLL or native implementation is loaded.</p>\n<p><code>--view front|rear</code> and <code>--zoom N</code> set a reproducible initial orbit camera. These options and <code>--animation</code> are for model inspection, not flight mode. The camera initially uses bind-pose bounds so two frame captures have identical framing. R resets the orbit around the current pose. Add <code>--snapshot path.ppm</code> to capture a chosen pose without opening a window.</p>\n<p>Manual clip/frame selection is separate from flight animation. Direct Sim bindings expose variable, units, scale, bias and optional lag. Supported XAPC expressions use the bounded interpreter. Static inspection remains read-only; temporal animation also supports model-local numeric data and scratch registers. Flight currently supplies physical center-wheel steering, throttle lever position and on-ground state; unknown values remain unavailable. Control-surface percent mappings, RPM, Standard autoplay timing/randomization and skin deformation still require work. Other clips remain in bind pose; mutually exclusive propeller and wheel alternatives can still be visible together. A clip with no geometry tracks is reported as such and has no visual effect.</p>\n<p>Local C172 evidence: 28 groups decode, 27 with rotation/translation tracks. Elevator frames 0 and 100 rendered through the same Vulkan path with identical camera framing; differences were confined to the tail region. Native <strong>]</strong> input advanced frame 50 to 55 and presented the updated geometry. This proves the inspected path, not FSX animation parity for all aircraft or channels.</p>\n<p>In flight mode, Q/E steering now updates C172 clip 4 automatically from the same CFG-derived angle used in wheel contact forces. The temporal evaluator uses simulation elapsed time, so pause holds visual state and R reinitializes it. Positive Lag bounds motion in authored keyframes per second. Unknown targets hold the last available pose; the first available target initializes immediately. These policies and the steering direction still lack an original-FSX comparison.</p>\n<p>Run the local-content controller-to-GPU check without opening a window:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example flight-pose-check -- ./FSX target/flight-pose-check</code></pre>\n<p>The output directory must be new. The check drives the shared flight keyboard controller directly, advances physics, evaluates the native animation, saves center/right/left/full-power P6 renders, and verifies pause/reset. It sends no OS input. Its fixed model-space camera isolates deformation from aircraft translation. The tested C172 CFG has 22 degrees maximum center steering: controller values 0 and +/-0.5 produce angles 0 and +/-11 degrees, or +/-12.222222 grads. Its native XAPC selects frames 200, 6.111111 and 193.88889. The two turning captures differ at 498 pixels within (591,469)..(617,502). This verifies the independent physics-to-rendering path, not FSX steering fidelity.</p>\n<h2>Model-local throttle expression and cockpit check</h2>\n<p>The C172 throttle XAPC uses L: variables, s0/l0 scratch storage and a local write. Temporal evaluation now resolves it from the independent throttle state: idle selects native frame 50 and full power selects frame 0, with the same value stored in Engine1ThrottlePosition (percent). The exterior resolves two of 28 groups; an exterior LOD-150 check showed no visible throttle pixel difference from the external camera. That result alone does not prove visible movement.</p>\n<p>For a direct view of the control, use the same controller-to-GPU check with the installed stock C172 interior model:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example flight-pose-check -- ./FSX target/cockpit-throttle-check --interior</code></pre>\n<p>This local validation option selects cessna172sp_interior.mdl, its LOD 100 (24,842 triangles, 55 scene nodes, 42 animation groups), and a fixed camera aimed at the throttle. Its eye [-0.25908, 0.57912, -0.10668] metres is derived from the observed stock C172 CFG datum/eyepoint; target [0, -0.1, 0.5] is chosen for this inspection, not FSX default-camera parity. One of 42 groups resolves. Seven of nine diffuse maps load (18 MiB decoded); dynamic $C172S_1/$C172S_2 panel surfaces are unavailable, so gauges remain blank. This control-inspection example is separate from the flight camera below; it does not implement live gauges or panel interaction.</p>\n<p>The verified idle/full-power renders differ at 1,908 pixels confined to the throttle region (397,166)..(447,222). Pose and local values follow model code; pause holds state even if a power key changes the pending control, and reset reinitializes the local context. Program writes commit only on valid results. Registers are temporary; local values are shared between this instance&#39;s clips, with matching units required. Other-aircraft/gauge sharing and original-FSX timing, scope and control-position comparison remain unverified.</p>\n<h2>Cockpit flight camera</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172&quot; --fly --ground --cockpit --windows-1252</code></pre>\n<p>Omit --ground for the 90 kt / 300 m airborne start. F9 selects/recenters the virtual cockpit and F11 returns to the exterior chase view. The default FSX Standard.xml maps F9/F11 to VIEW_CAMERA_SELECT_1/3; the installed cameras.cfg assigns those slots to Virtual Cockpit/Locked Spot. Those associations were checked locally; original files are not copied into openFSX. The prototype&#39;s exterior camera remains its own chase view, not FSX Locked Spot behavior.</p>\n<p>The loader reads [models] interior from the selected variant&#39;s model.cfg, and [views] eyepoint plus reference_datum_position from aircraft.cfg. Feet convert to body forward/right/down metres, then to the same loaded-CG/world transform as the aircraft mesh. Camera up follows aircraft attitude, including bank. Missing interior/eyepoint disables cockpit selection; --cockpit reports an error. Invalid vectors and non-component model references fail explicitly.</p>\n<p>In cockpit view, left drag looks around and the wheel changes vertical FOV between 15 and 90 degrees; F9 recenters at a nominal 45 degrees. This free-look gesture, initial FOV, camera definitions, InitialZoom/InitialPbh, head movement and clip behavior have not been matched to FSX. The near plane is 0.01 m. The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/utilities/camera_configuration/camera_configuration.html\">camera SDK</a> describes the broader system; the current camera implements only this subset. Dynamic gauge surfaces are still blank and panel clicks are not implemented. Use --debug-hud if experimental telemetry is needed. The outside world remains the original test plane/runway, with no FSX scenery loaded.</p>\n<p>Both visual models retain and update their animation states. Selection exchanges mesh/material resources on the existing GPU device and surface; it does not recreate the window or reset physics. --lod applies to the starting model; the alternate model initially selects its highest available LOD. A failed optional alternate load leaves the starting view usable, with a diagnostic.</p>\n<p>For a reproducible check without a window or OS input:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example flight-view-check -- ./FSX target/flight-view-check</code></pre>\n<p>The output directory must be new. The shared camera/resource-exchange code was checked at ground start, level flight and after one second of right-bank input. The last state reports 90.1 kt, 300 m and 15.7 degrees right bank. Its horizon tilts while cockpit geometry stays attached to the aircraft. For all three states, exterior -&gt; cockpit -&gt; exterior produces byte-identical exterior GPU readbacks and leaves simulation time, position, orientation and throttle unchanged. The common projection now uses the native Direct3D/MDLX handedness, correcting previously mirrored left/right geometry and bank direction. All published GPU captures were rerendered after this correction. Release CLI --cockpit snapshots passed for one-second ground and ten-second airborne runs. These checks verify the shared rendering path; native F9/F11 input has been wired and compiled but was not injected into a desktop window during this verification.</p>\n<h2>Reference calibration</h2>\n<p><code>tools/fsx-reference</code> contains a separate Windows x86 reader for the original FSX process. Build it with <code>./scripts/build-reference.ps1</code>, load a stock C172 flight in FSX and capture with:</p>\n<pre><code>./target/fsx-reference.exe --fsx &quot;./FSX&quot; --output target/fsx-controls.ndjson --seconds 30</code></pre>\n<p>This reads surface angles, percentages, trim, yoke, throttle, on-ground state and aircraft title. It sends no control events. Record exact loading/settings and the input sequence alongside each local capture. Synthetic protocol checks pass. A loaded local C172 flight returned 87 valid packets over five seconds and another 35 over two seconds on 18 October 2026. User-aircraft request alias 0 resolved to actual object ID 1, which the reader validates and binds. This establishes baseline acquisition; control sweeps and angle/percentage calibration remain unverified. A failed connection or an empty output is not calibration evidence. Flight mode has an initial center-steering binding; control-surface mapping remains uncalibrated. Offline pose import is available for supported recorded bindings, as described below.</p>\n<h2>Recorded instrument indications</h2>\n<p>The separate reference reader accepts <code>--instruments</code> to add 32 air-data, gyro, clock, loading, engine and instrument-state readings to the existing 12 controls in one response packet. This schema-2 live request is not yet verified against a running FSX. Default schema-1 capture remains available.</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-reference.ps1\n./target/fsx-reference.exe --fsx ./FSX --output target/fsx-instruments.ndjson --seconds 30 --instruments</code></pre>\n<p>Load the matching C172 variant-0 flight first and record its settings. Source labels alone do not prove a file&#39;s authenticity. To validate or replay a schema-2 recording through the checked C172 native panel profile:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example reference-gauge-replay -- --validate target/fsx-instruments.ndjson\n./scripts/build-gauge-host.ps1\ncargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example reference-gauge-replay -- ./FSX target/fsx-instruments.ndjson target/reference-gauge-replay</code></pre>\n<p>Replay streams bounded records and preserves the absolute simulator clock&#39;s pause/rewind behavior independently of wall time. It uses actual recorded IAS, indicated altitude, VSI and gyro readings as native inputs. Failed/caged or adjusted instruments and changing values on an unchanged simulator clock are not supported by this profile. Real-source titles must match the loaded variant. Schema-2 files can also supply the existing <code>--reference-pose</code> inspector, with explicit unit conversions and their original source/title labels.</p>\n<p>Available stateless model-animation bindings also consume recorded control and instrument variables; unsupported expressions and positive lag remain omitted. The new output directory contains fixed-camera first/last images, per-frame native callback results and an explicitly non-parity diagnostic report. ISA, body/gyro and pressure differences describe assumptions to check against future real data. Aircraft trajectory, lag and complete instrument state are not replayed.</p>\n<p>The C# synthetic fixture passed the Rust validator and native render path. A five-record synthetic sequence exercised one pause reuse and one rewind/helper restart; its 600 fpm reading returned 600.0000192 through the native VSI callback. No original FSX process was running during this check. See the measurement tool&#39;s repository README for the complete schema and native-profile limits.</p>\n<h2>Inspect one captured pose</h2>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --reference-pose target/fsx-controls.ndjson --reference-sample 0 --view rear --zoom 1.6</code></pre>\n<p>The sample index is zero-based. Import validates schema 1, the selected row&#39;s 12 finite control values, aircraft title, time and source label. Schema 2 additionally requires its 32 finite instrument fields and object/index metadata. Files are limited to 64 MiB, rows to 16 KiB and indexes to fewer than 100,000. The window title and console identify the declared source and sample. Use the matching aircraft MDL; the tool prints the recorded title but cannot prove that it matches an arbitrary model file. Source labels are metadata, not proof of a capture&#39;s authenticity.</p>\n<p>Sample 0 from the locally recorded 87-packet C172 baseline was imported and rendered offscreen. Its seven available XAPS control/trim bindings resolve to frame 50. All recorded control readings were neutral, so this verifies the live acquisition-to-pose pipeline without establishing motion, signs or calibration.</p>\n<p>The adapter resolves known variables in their requested units, then uses the model&#39;s own XAPS scale and bias to choose keyframes. Percent and percent-over-100 conversion and degree/radian conversion are explicit. Unknown variables/units, unsupported code, positive lag and standard autoplay are skipped with diagnostics. Shared transform IDs compose in file order; full multi-clip FSX equivalence remains unverified. This is one static pose, not temporal replay or flight input. It cannot be combined with --fly or manual --animation/--frame. Add --snapshot for offscreen output through the same renderer.</p>\n<p>For variables outside the recorded schema, repeat <code>--pose-var NAME#units#value</code>. This supplies explicit inspection values, not FSX measurements, and resolves both XAPS and supported read-only XAPC expressions. The source code chooses authored frames; no manual frame mapping is added for individual aircraft. The option cannot be combined with flight, a recorded pose or manual animation. Up to 128 distinct variable/unit pairs are allowed, with finite f32-range values.</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --pose-var &quot;GEAR CENTER STEER ANGLE#grads#40&quot; --visibility-var &quot;PROP MAX RPM PERCENT:1#percent#0&quot; --view front --zoom 1.6 --snapshot target/c172-steer.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>On the local C172, explicit steering values +40 and -40 in the model-requested grads unit resolve XAPC clip 4 to frames 20 and 180. Offscreen GPU snapshots from the same camera differ at 516 pixels, confined to the nose-wheel region (593,470)..(617,502). This verifies the native expression-to-geometry path; it does not establish steering calibration or a match to an FSX reference frame. The menu now supplies zero steering to this same pose evaluator for its stationary preview. That menu wiring is compiled; the changed native menu has not been reopened for visual verification during this check.</p>\n<p>For a reproducible pipeline check without an FSX server:</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-reference.ps1\ncargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --reference-pose target/reference-synthetic.ndjson --view rear --zoom 1.6 --snapshot target/c172-reference-synthetic.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>The helper generates this original test sample with source <strong>synthetic</strong>; it contains invented values, not FSX measurements. On the local C172 it resolves seven of 28 animation groups and renders the posed aircraft. This checks the C# encoder, JSON import, unit adapter, authored key evaluation and GPU path. It does not establish physical calibration or FSX animation parity.</p>\n<p>Earlier Remote Desktop and menu-only attempts returned no aircraft samples. The later successful captures above required a loaded local C172 flight; the offline openFSX pipeline does not launch FSX.</p>\n<h2>Experimental live native C172 needles</h2>\n<p>Windows flight mode can opt into the five checked native needle bindings:</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-gauge-host.ps1\ncargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- ./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172 --fly --cockpit --native-gauges --windows-1252</code></pre>\n<p>Add <code>--xml-gauge-font C:/Windows/Fonts/arial.ttf</code> to draw the XML registration plaque using that explicitly selected installed font. No font is bundled or silently chosen for the XML&#39;s missing FontFace. This option uses authored atc_id, the current daylight environment and panel-light value false. FontHeight uses pixels per em; FSX default-font/GDI metrics and filtering remain unverified. The normal native profile keeps its XML omission unless a font is supplied.</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --example xml-text-render-check -- FSX target/xml-text-check C:/Windows/Fonts/arial.ttf\ncargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --example native-gauge-flight-check -- FSX target/xml-flight-check target/fsx-gauge-host.exe C:/Windows/Fonts/arial.ttf</code></pre>\n<p>Both output directories must be new. The first example checks two authored registrations and two synthetic text-color conditions through GPU snapshots; both use the authored day background and do not simulate cockpit night lighting. The second checks XML persistence with live native needles/sprites, pause/reset, view changes and helper closure. Neither launches original FSX.</p>\n<p>The helper defaults to <code>fsx-gauge-host.exe</code> beside the viewer or in its parent build directory. Use <code>--gauge-host PATH</code> to select it explicitly. This option requires Windows, flight mode, textures and the checked C172 VC placements. It does not enable instruments in the menu&#39;s FLY NOW launch path.</p>\n<p>A persistent, hidden x86 process loads the installed Cessna/Cessna172/Bell_206B DLLs, calls ModuleInit once and updates the airspeed, vertical-speed and three altimeter needles. The independent flight model supplies ideal ISA calibrated airspeed in knots, pressure altitude in feet at the standard pressure setting, and vertical speed in the legacy 1/256 m/s token units. This assumes subsonic flow and local ISA height between 0 and 11,000 m; unsupported data fails explicitly. Instrument errors, weather and adjustable pressure settings are not modeled. The renderer paints native needle bitmaps over decoded static panel artwork using authored pivots and curves, and updates the existing GPU textures without replacing scene geometry.</p>\n<p>The same option draws the native heading card and both attitude sprites with the original RES_ID+1 masks and foreground artwork. Ideal gyro pitch/bank and local heading come from the aircraft orientation; legacy inputs are nose-down and left-bank positive. Original token-78 pitch and token-84 heading callbacks run in the helper; bank axes use their encoded direct source. Authored scales apply once, with rotation before texture scaling and source translation. Pixels use nearest-neighbor sampling of the installed source bitmap. RGB zero in a mask preserves the preceding layer, RGB (1,1,1) opens the moving texture, and other colors draw stationary foreground. Reticle and adjustment artwork use neutral authored placement. This ideal gyro profile has no magnetic variation, drift, failures, erection dynamics or knob adjustment.</p>\n<p>Callbacks run only when simulation time advances. Pause reuses results; a backward clock reset closes/deinitializes the old modules and starts a fresh helper. F9/F11 preserve the latest interior textures, including updates while viewing the exterior. Normal exit deinitializes all three modules; protocol errors, disconnects and callback timeouts terminate the helper and report failure.</p>\n<p>Run the installed-content integration check in a new output directory:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-app --features viewer --example native-gauge-flight-check -- ./FSX target/native-gauge-flight-local</code></pre>\n<p>On 19 October 2026 the local RTX 2080/Vulkan check passed 121 forward frames, ten paused result reuses, dynamic GPU texture updates, exterior/interior round trips, helper restart and identical initial/reset pixels. With the camera and geometry fixed, flight-driven needles changed 1,706 RGB bytes. The settled ground airspeed was 0.00136 kt, matching residual physics velocity. These are openFSX consistency checks, not recorded FSX instrument comparisons. Native window input with this option has not been rechecked.</p>\n<p>The extended integration check subsequently passed all three flight-driven sprites, 121 forward frames and ten pause reuses. Frozen camera/geometry pixels changed in 12,161 RGB bytes during combined pitch/roll input; reset and view round trips retained identical expected pixels. A separate explicit-artwork check saves level, nose-up/down, right-bank and east-heading images:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run --locked -p fsx-render --example native-sprite-check -- ./FSX target/native-sprite-local</code></pre>\n<p>Visual inspection confirmed that nose-up lowers the horizon, right bank tilts it left, and heading 90 places E under the top index. Local source art is 84/93 pixels wide; openFSX samples those bitmaps directly, without a 256-pixel runtime texture conversion. FSX filtering and subpixel placement remain unverified.</p>\n<p>RPM, other moving faces, broader digital/XML gauges, needle lag, interaction and the full PANELS/gauge lifecycle remain unsupported. Static root artwork can display fixed or empty regions for those instruments; it must not be treated as operational telemetry. Native DLL code remains in the separate helper; the x64 renderer does not load it.</p>\n<p>Research references: <a href=\"https://prepar3d.com/SDKv4/sdk/panels_and_user_interface/gauges/references/gauges_api_overview.html\">gauge API</a>, <a href=\"https://prepar3d.com/SDKv4/sdk/panels_and_user_interface/gauges/references/gauges.html\">published ABI declarations</a> and <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.org/SDKv4/sdk/panels_and_user_interface/panels/panel_and_gauge_artwork.html\">gauge artwork conventions</a>. These later SDK references informed independent readers and rendering; local FSX resources and synthetic tests establish the checked subset.</p>\n<h2>Passive reference screenshots</h2>\n<p>The independent <code>tools/fsx-capture</code> Windows utility captures only the selected game window through Windows Graphics Capture. It never activates or restores the window or sends mouse/keyboard input. Leave FSX unminimized behind other windows; minimized windows are rejected without restoring them. Windows capture indicators and security policy keep their defaults; there is no desktop fallback.</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-capture.ps1\n./target/fsx-capture.exe --pid 12345 --list\n./target/fsx-capture.exe --pid 12345 --output target/fsx-reference.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>Replace <code>12345</code> with the running game&#39;s process ID. The build needs installed Visual Studio C++ tools and a Windows SDK with C++/WinRT headers. Output must be a new file. Local compilation and synthetic pixel/dimension checks pass. A 1024x738 passive capture of the openFSX menu was visually verified, including the native 3D preview; the foreground-window handle was unchanged. Original-FSX cockpit capture remains unverified: it was minimized during the first inspection, and the later available game window belonged to openFSX. Keep original-game images under ignored <code>target/</code> as reference material. They are not screenshots of openFSX progress.</p>\n<h2>Visibility inspection</h2>\n<p>VISL conditions link to scene nodes through SGVL. The reader supports direct VISS/VINS variable reads and a numeric, read-only VISC/VINC RPN subset: finite numbers, A-variable reads with explicit units, arithmetic, comparisons, boolean operators and lazy nested if/else branches. Defaults encoded by the file apply to unavailable variables, unsupported operations and invalid evaluation, with diagnostics. Hiding a parent hides its descendants. Draw masks preserve vertex buffers, material ranges and framing; both opaque and blended draws honor them.</p>\n<p>Supply exact variable/unit values for inspection, for example:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/model/cessna172sp.mdl&quot; --lod 150 --texture-dir &quot;./FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172/texture.1&quot; --visibility-var &quot;PROP MAX RPM PERCENT:1#percent#0&quot; --view front --zoom 1.6 --snapshot target/c172-visibility-stopped.ppm</code></pre>\n<p>Repeat --visibility-var for different variables; at most 128 unique name/unit pairs are accepted. Matching is ASCII case-insensitive, without inferred unit conversion. Values must be finite. Set the example value to 8 or 20 to inspect the intermediate or blurred propeller alternative. These are explicit inputs, not captured RPM. The option cannot override --fly or --menu; a selected mask is preserved while manually scrubbing animation keys.</p>\n<p>The menu supplies a stationary, engine-stopped inspection state: propeller percent and wheel rotation rates zero, landing lights off. On the local C172, all 61 conditions resolve and seven LOD-400 draw batches are hidden. This is a presentation state, not a simulation of propeller mechanics or FSX menu parity. Flight still lacks engine/wheel RPM state, so unknown conditions retain their encoded defaults. Model visibility does not implement gauge XML, local variables, assignment/events, registers, loops, strings, native gauge DLLs or temporal visibility lag. Evaluation must leave exactly one finite scalar result. The RPN interpreter bounds source size (16 KiB), instructions (4096), nesting (32) and stack entries (64), and never executes native or original gauge code. A model visibility block is limited to 4 MiB and 262,144 compiled instructions in total.</p>"
  },
  "ARCHITECTURE": {
    "source": "docs/ARCHITECTURE.md",
    "title": "Architecture",
    "html": "<h1>Architecture</h1>\n<p>openFSX is an independent runtime for a user&#39;s installed FSX content. Original executable code and game resources are not distributed. The openOMSI approach is an inspiration; its implementation is not copied.</p>\n<h2>Workspace layout</h2>\n<p>The repository follows the subsystem-workspace layout of <a href=\"https://github.com/openOMSI-org/openOMSI\">openOMSI</a>: engine packages under <code>crates/</code>, developer tools under <code>tools/</code>, launcher frontend under <code>launcher/</code>, shared runtime resources under <code>assets/</code>, and documentation/site/build scripts at the root. The implementation and FSX-specific boundaries are independent. There are no placeholder crates for unimplemented scenery, gauges or APIs.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Package</th><th scope=\"col\">Responsibility</th><th scope=\"col\">Internal dependencies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>fsx-cfg</td><td>CFG parsing and explicit code-page decoding</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-geometry</td><td>Matrices, camera math and double-precision rigid orientation</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-script</td><td>Bounded numeric RPN, read-only GaugeString and optional model-local data</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-model</td><td>MDLX geometry, materials, scene graph, authored keys and visibility</td><td>fsx-geometry, fsx-script</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-texture</td><td>DDS top-mip and BI_RGB bitmap decoding, texture.cfg fallback lookup</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-vehicle</td><td>CFG/AIR data, static loading, contacts, panel placements, native gauge metadata and CAB/ACE XML documents</td><td>fsx-cfg, fsx-geometry</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-sim</td><td>6DOF integration, aerodynamics and wheel contact forces</td><td>fsx-vehicle, fsx-geometry</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-render</td><td>wgpu resources, materials, draw passes, XML text rasterization, debugging overlay and readback</td><td>model, texture, geometry, vehicle, sim, script</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-ui</td><td>Windows WebView2 menu host, native 3D preview and bounded installation asset access</td><td>fsx-cfg, fsx-model, fsx-vehicle, fsx-render</td></tr><tr><td>fsx-app</td><td>Native event loop, keyboard flight adapter and view orchestration</td><td>content, sim, render, UI</td></tr><tr><td>tools/fsx-check</td><td>Headless openfsx CLI and repeatable local content/flight checks</td><td>cfg, model, vehicle, sim</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The root Cargo.toml is a virtual workspace with shared versions/dependencies. Its default member is fsx-check, preserving <code>cargo run -- inspect ...</code>. The separate <code>tools/fsx-reference</code> Windows x86 measurement helper reads an original running FSX through its installed client. It is outside the engine&#39;s Cargo graph; no subsystem links the original SimConnect DLL. Protocol checks use original synthetic packets and run in Windows CI without game resources.</p>\n<p>The separate C++ tool under tools/fsx-gauge-host is an experimental Windows x86 process for isolated native needle-update calls. It loads only the explicitly selected installed stock library; Rust engine packages do not link to it or to FSX import libraries. Its own synthetic ABI/exception/protocol self-test runs in Windows CI without game files. Local native probes do not implement the PANELS API or a full gauge lifecycle and are not connected to flight-mode instruments. The Rust content reader remains read-only and never executes DLLs. Build the native application with an explicit package:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --bin openfsx-viewer -- --help\ncargo test --workspace --all-features --locked\nnode scripts/check-architecture.mjs</code></pre>\n<p><code>launcher/index.html</code> is embedded by fsx-ui. Owned WGSL shaders under <code>assets/shaders/</code> are embedded by fsx-render. The local <code>FSX/</code> installation remains ignored and outside all workspace packages and public site roots. The Windows helper <code>scripts/build-windows.cmd</code> builds both release binaries under <code>target/release/</code>; it does not package game content.</p>\n<h2>Dependency boundaries</h2>\n<p>File readers and vehicle definitions do not depend on the simulator, windowing or GPU. Vehicle contact configuration computes static spring parameters; the runtime force law is a local extension trait in fsx-sim. Aerodynamic methods likewise extend immutable vehicle parameters inside fsx-sim. Shared rigid orientation lives in fsx-geometry, avoiding a vehicle/simulation cycle.</p>\n<p>The renderer consumes decoded content and readout data. It owns no application event loop or pilot input. fsx-app connects the selected aircraft, fixed-step session, renderer and menu. fsx-sim has no renderer or UI dependency.</p>\n<p><code>scripts/architecture.json</code> declares allowed internal dependency directions; <code>node scripts/check-architecture.mjs</code> checks Cargo metadata and source roots. CI runs the guard along with workspace tests and the native build. Adding a subsystem requires an explicit layer rule. Original synthetic aircraft fixtures are exposed only by fsx-vehicle&#39;s test-support feature for cross-crate tests.</p>\n<h2>Current coverage</h2>\n<p>The crates reflect implemented boundaries, not completed FSX subsystems. Content decoding, rendering, manual key inspection and flight remain partial. Earth coordinates, airport/BGL terrain streaming, XML gauges, SimVars, SimConnect and native add-on bridges are still unimplemented. Compatibility claims must follow the evidence in the <a href=\"#/docs/COMPATIBILITY\">compatibility record</a>.</p>\n<h2>Rendering boundary</h2>\n<p>The MDLX reader produces validated world-space triangles for one LOD. It applies the static TRAN/AMAP/SCEN hierarchy. Animated nodes stay in bind pose by default; explicit manual clip/frame inspection evaluates authored rotation and translation keys. A bounded numeric RPN subset evaluates XAPC animation frame expressions and VISL conditions through an application-provided variable lookup; SGVL visibility inherits through scene parents and masks draw calls without changing topology. Unavailable or unsupported conditions keep their encoded defaults with diagnostics. Per-model temporal state limits supported animation targets by their Lag rate on the simulation clock. Visibility and static inspection use a read-only mode. Temporal XAPC evaluation can additionally use a bounded per-instance local-variable context and temporary registers; only valid expression/frame results commit changes. This is not shared XML-gauge state or an event/API bridge. The application visual adapter derives center steering from the same physical wheel-angle calculation as contact forces, and drives the native throttle expression from throttle lever state. It also exposes the on-ground flag. Geometry is rebuilt only when authored frames or inherited visibility change; pause holds time and reset clears the visual state. Skinning is not evaluated. Omitted skinned parts are counted; complete visual compatibility must not be claimed.</p>\n<p>The renderer uses a depth buffer, diffuse DDS maps, material RGB fallback and face lighting. Opaque parts write depth first; ordinary source-alpha/inverse-source-alpha parts are drawn back to front by part center without depth writes. Alpha tests are evaluated separately from blending. This is approximate transparency, not parity with FSX material rendering. Normal/specular maps, reflection, effects and full aircraft visual state remain future work. Offscreen snapshots use the same pipeline as the window. The renderer can replace scene vertices, material bindings and textures on the existing device/surface for flight-view changes. The host caches exterior/interior content and poses independently, with one shared physics session. A validated explicit eye/target/up projection lets the cockpit camera follow pitch and bank from the loaded-CG body transform. Variant model.cfg interior and aircraft.cfg eyepoint metadata remain in the headless vehicle layer; view gestures and selection stay in the application. Camera-profile overlays, FSX zoom matching and live gauge surfaces remain incomplete.</p>\n<p>Texture lookup starts in the selected livery folder and follows numerically ordered texture.cfg fallback entries, with cycle detection and a directory limit. DDS is decoded on the CPU to RGBA8 before GPU upload. Only referenced diffuse textures are loaded; non-diffuse maps are not mistakenly treated as albedo. Missing or unsupported files produce diagnostics and a material-color fallback.</p>\n<h2>Native instrument boundary</h2>\n<p>The optional application adapter binds five checked C172 needles to independent ISA air data in fsx-sim and three attitude/heading sprites to ideal gyro orientation. It communicates over bounded stdio with a hidden Windows x86 helper; original DLLs never enter the x64 viewer. The helper validates each native element/token/callback address, maintains module lifetime and invokes callbacks only when simulation time advances. Pause reuses results and rewind starts fresh modules. The app validates ordered response bindings and paints decoded bitmap needles and authored sprite/mask pairs; fsx-render updates named, dimension-checked texture pixels on the existing GPU. Cached interior images remain current in exterior view. The headless simulation contains no process or gauge-host dependency. Full PANELS services, gauge lifecycle stages and broad unit/event contracts remain future work.</p>\n<h2>Reference measurement boundary</h2>\n<p>The independent x86 C# reader can optionally request 12 controls and 32 instrument/flight-state datums in a single SimConnect response. Schema-2 NDJSON preserves absolute simulator time, wall time, aircraft title, user-object ID, sequence and explicit unit choices. The application streams these records with size/count/identity checks; existing schema-1 pose import remains supported. Offline gauge replay feeds recorded indications to the isolated native host, normalizes simulation time and starts fresh native state after rewinds. Its fixed-camera images and assumption-labeled ISA/gyro differences establish a reproduction/diagnostic path, not flight-physics or instrument parity. CI validates C# serialization against the Rust reader without game files. No original client or measurement dependency enters the headless simulation.</p>\n<h2>Milestones and acceptance criteria</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Inventory.</strong> CFG encodings, external SimObjects roots and actionable errors. Current coverage is titles in the standard Airplanes directory.</li><li><strong>Aircraft viewer.</strong> Display one verified MDLX variant and its textures without FSX. A C172 livery has been rendered with available diffuse maps. Generated registration decals, animation, visibility and skinning remain incomplete; the result is a viewer baseline, not complete visual compatibility.</li><li><strong>First flight.</strong> Airborne and runway C172 starts use AIR/CFG parameters, keyboard controls, RK4 integration and original flat-ground geometry. CFG payload/full usable fuel determine initial mass, CG and a full loaded inertia tensor. Three fixed-wheel spring/damper contacts allow parking, braking, steering, ground roll and departure. Speed/climb/post-peak-lift and ground/departure scenarios are repeatable; recorded FSX reference comparisons, engine tables, fuel consumption, scrape contact and landing validation remain incomplete. See the flight guide for evidence and limitations.</li><li><strong>Local scenery.</strong> One airport, an explicit BGL subset, terrain and streaming. Record format coverage and frame-time measurements.</li><li><strong>Cockpit and APIs.</strong> A tested subset of XML gauges and SimConnect contracts. Native DLL/GAU add-ons need a separate decision: 32-bit code cannot simply be loaded into a 64-bit process.</li></ol>\n<p>No deadlines or universal compatibility guarantees are stated. Each milestone needs original test data and measurable results.</p>"
  },
  "COMPATIBILITY": {
    "source": "docs/COMPATIBILITY.md",
    "title": "Compatibility",
    "html": "<h1>Compatibility</h1>\n<p>The project target is FSX 1:1 across interface, behavior, content and APIs. No subsystem currently has a complete recorded parity comparison.</p>\n<p>This page records observed behavior, not a promise that existing add-ons will work in a future simulator.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Area</th><th scope=\"col\">Current support</th><th scope=\"col\">Limits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FSX-style menu</td><td>Initial Windows host</td><td>Local UI skin, aircraft catalog, live native MDLX/DDS preview, launch and return; independent layout, no pixel-parity verification; other sections unimplemented</td></tr><tr><td>Installation</td><td>Directory structure check</td><td>fsx.exe and standard Airplanes folder</td></tr><tr><td>aircraft.cfg</td><td>Partial</td><td>Variant references, geometry, station payload, full usable Avgas loading, loaded CG/inertia tensor and engine/control parameters; explicit encoding</td></tr><tr><td>MDLX</td><td>Partial</td><td>Packed chunks, VERT, INDE, PART, LOD, static TRAN/AMAP/SCEN</td></tr><tr><td>Native viewer</td><td>Experimental</td><td>Diffuse textured geometry, approximate alpha blending; manual native-key inspection</td></tr><tr><td>MDLX animation keys</td><td>Initial runtime binding subset</td><td>ANIB/XANI/XANS/XANK and SGAL; XAPS/XAPC, bounded instance-local data/registers, simulation-clock Lag and automatic center steering/throttle; control-surface percent mappings, RPM, Standard autoplay and full FSX timing remain unverified or unimplemented</td></tr><tr><td>Cockpit flight view</td><td>Initial native-interior subset</td><td>Variant model.cfg interior and CFG eyepoint, attached pitch/bank camera, F9/F11 selection and existing-device resource exchange; offscreen ground/air/bank and release CLI checked; native key input, FSX camera definitions/FOV parity, default gauges and panel interaction remain unimplemented; opt-in five-needle C172 profile below</td></tr><tr><td>panel.cfg</td><td>Read-only placement subset</td><td>Variant-selected panel path, Window/VCockpit indices, titles, gauge references/coordinates/optional dimensions/parameters and case-insensitive model texture bindings; backgrounds, aliases, layout behavior and gauge execution remain unimplemented</td></tr><tr><td>MDLX visibility</td><td>Initial read-only subset</td><td>VISL VISS/VINS/VISC/VINC and SGVL; numeric RPN, A-variable lookup and inherited draw masks; unsupported or unavailable conditions use encoded defaults</td></tr><tr><td>Skinned MDLX parts</td><td>Omitted with a count</td><td>No bone deformation</td></tr><tr><td>MDL8, MDL9, P3D</td><td>Unsupported</td><td>Explicit format error</td></tr><tr><td>DDS diffuse maps</td><td>Partial</td><td>DXT1/3/5 and masked 32-bit RGB, top mip only; up to 4096 pixels per dimension</td></tr><tr><td>Texture lookup</td><td>Partial</td><td>Selected folder and ordered texture.cfg fallback graph; ASCII case-insensitive file lookup</td></tr><tr><td>BMP / native bitmap resources</td><td>Partial</td><td>Ordinary BI_RGB 4/8/24-bit plus explicit XML Alpha mode for 32-bit BGRA BMP files; palettes, padding, orientation and pixel offsets. Native DIB alpha, RLE, bitfields, extended headers and Luminous application remain unsupported</td></tr><tr><td>Advanced materials</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>No bump, specular, reflection or emissive maps</td></tr><tr><td>Static panel artwork inspection</td><td>Separate offscreen example</td><td>28 C172 root images bound to the two native VC textures; no moving layers, needles, digital values, XML, interaction or automatic flight-mode panel loading</td></tr><tr><td>Flight dynamics / AIR</td><td>Initial airborne subset</td><td>Legacy 1101/0404/0473; modern overrides explicitly rejected; approximate engine</td></tr><tr><td>Keyboard flight mode</td><td>Experimental</td><td>Airborne C172 start or fixed-wheel runway start; power, brakes, steering and departure checked</td></tr><tr><td>Wheel contact</td><td>Initial subset</td><td>Three fixed wheels, flat-plane springs/dampers and regularized friction; non-wheel contacts omitted, no landing-performance validation</td></tr><tr><td>Flight overlay</td><td>Optional --debug-hud</td><td>Live TAS, local height/heading, vertical speed, commands, attitude and status; independent of FSX gauges</td></tr><tr><td>BGL / scenery</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>No airport or terrain runtime</td></tr><tr><td>XML gauges</td><td>Opt-in C172 static-background/text subset</td><td>CAB/ACE, read-only string/numeric/conditional GaugeString, parented Element Visibility, authored atc_id and explicit local-font rasterization via --xml-gauge-font; single-line ASCII/daylight flight profile. Numeric/visibility rendering has synthetic checks; electrical systems, operational radios, FSX font/rounding, night lighting, shared state and interaction remain unimplemented or unverified</td></tr><tr><td>Native gauge DLL/GAU files</td><td>Read-only metadata and bitmap subset</td><td>x86 PE32 Linkage, FS800/900/1000 prefixes, element graphs, needle/sprite/string descriptors and localized resources; Rust content reading never loads or executes a DLL</td></tr><tr><td>Native needle update functions</td><td>Isolated Windows x86 diagnostic and opt-in C172 stream</td><td>Five stock DLL load probes and 18 explicit raw-input callback samples; persistent ModuleInit/ModuleDeinit lifetime, five air-data needles and three gyro sprites, pause reuse and reset restart; no PANELS services or full gauge lifecycle</td></tr><tr><td>Native string update functions</td><td>Isolated explicit-input probe</td><td>Three Nav-Comm BCD samples checked with token/type/value echoes and guarded ASCII output; no operational radios, GDI font autosizing or flight-mode native text. Clock/private-state and transponder failures remain unsupported</td></tr><tr><td>Native needle artwork</td><td>Recorded-value inspection and five live C172 needles</td><td>Six original bitmap needles in the offscreen inspector; opt-in flight airspeed/VSI/three altimeter hands update existing GPU textures; authored pivots/tables, no lag, full panel behavior or default flight integration</td></tr><tr><td>Native gyro sprites</td><td>Opt-in C172 heading card and two attitude sprites</td><td>Native token-78/84 callbacks and direct bank readings, authored masks/scales and neutral fixed layers; no drift, failures, magnetic variation, adjustment controls or FSX filtering verification</td></tr><tr><td>Native gauge hosting / SimConnect server</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>Callback/API implementation and behavior comparisons remain required</td></tr><tr><td>Original-FSX measurement tool</td><td>Live schema-1 controls; schema-2 instrument profile prepared</td><td>Separate x86 reader; legacy C172 capture returned 87/35 packets; optional 32 instrument/clock/loading/state fields share one packet; extended live request unverified, synthetic C#/Rust interoperability checked</td></tr><tr><td>Recorded instrument replay</td><td>Bounded offline C172 profile</td><td>Actual recorded indications drive five needles/three sprites and available stateless model bindings; simulation-clock pause/rewind checked on five synthetic records; fixed camera, no trajectory/lag/full instrument-state replay or FSX parity verdict</td></tr><tr><td>Passive reference screenshots</td><td>openFSX menu verified</td><td>Independent Windows Graphics Capture utility under tools/fsx-capture; 1024x738 menu and native 3D preview captured with foreground unchanged and no activation, restoration or input; original-FSX cockpit still unverified</td></tr><tr><td>Recorded pose inspection</td><td>Initial static subset</td><td>One schema-1 NDJSON sample drives available XAPS/read-only XAPC bindings; synthetic and locally captured neutral C172 samples rendered; no live calibration or temporal lag evaluation</td></tr><tr><td>Audio, AI, multiplayer</td><td>Not implemented</td><td>Future subsystems</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>Local evidence</h2>\n<p>On 19 October 2026 the opt-in C172 flight integration passed 121 forward frames, ten paused result reuses, dynamic GPU texture updates, view round trips and fresh module initialization after reset. Initial/reset cockpit pixels matched exactly. With fixed camera and geometry the moving native needles changed 1,706 RGB bytes. The ground result matched the 0.00136 kt residual physics velocity. Inputs use ideal ISA air data rather than weather or instrument-error models; the extended gyro check changed 12,161 RGB bytes and passed the same pause/reset/view gates. Original-FSX instrument comparisons and live window input with this option remain unverified. See <a href=\"#/docs/PORTING\">the porting checkpoint</a> and <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">reproduction instructions</a>.</p>\n<p>On 18 October 2026, <code>inspect-panel</code> read the installed C172 variant-0 configuration: six 2D windows with 147 placements and two virtual-cockpit panels with 29 placements. Both native interior texture names matched their configured panels case-insensitively. A321 (17 VC placements) and B747 (28 VC placements) also matched both configured textures. These are configuration/model checks, not instrument rendering or behavior validation. C208B inspection rejects its installed <code>pixel_size=512,512s</code> value; FSX&#39;s handling of that malformed value has not been measured. No installation file was corrected or included in the repository.</p>\n<p>The read-only native gauge check resolved 28 C172 VC placements against Cessna, Cessna172, Cessna182s, Bell_206B and Bendix_king_radio DLLs. The remaining placement names an XML gauge in n_number_plaque.cab. All positive element resource IDs in the resolved placements have matching bitmap resources. The five libraries contain 88 Linkage entries (including repeated header aliases) and 1,032 localized resource leaves. FS800 and FS1000 headers were observed locally; FS900 prefix coverage uses synthetic data. No callback ran and no original bitmap was published.</p>\n<p>The separate static-artwork example decoded all 1,022 bitmap leaves and seven ordinary panel BMP files, then drew 28 root images into $C172s_1/$C172s_2 using exact language 1033. The before/after snapshots share geometry, camera and GPU; visual inspection confirmed upright native scales and radio artwork. The image shows missing needles, moving instrument faces and digital values. The 108 non-root elements remain omitted; one XML placement is diagnosed. This verifies an artwork path, not functional gauges or FSX rasterization parity. Default flight-mode gauges remain blank.</p>\n<p>On 19 October 2026, the separate x86 gauge probe loaded all five stock libraries and recorded 18 native needle-update calls. Airspeed inputs 0/75/200 and tachometer inputs 0/1800/2700 were preserved by the native functions. Vertical-speed inputs -256/0/256 returned -196.8504/0/196.8504. For raw inputs 0/500/1000, altimeter resources 1031, 1032 and 1033 returned respectively -90/90/270, -90/-72/-54 and -90/-88.2/-86.4. ModuleInit and ModuleDeinit completed in each sample process. Inputs are synthetic inspection values, not recorded FSX flight data; callback results are not automatically interpreted as readings or angles. No PANELS services or operational instrument rendering was exercised.</p>\n<p>The native-needle-check example subsequently consumed those 18 results and rendered six original needles in three offscreen C172 interiors. Airspeed, tachometer and vertical speed use authored nonlinearity tables; the three selected Cessna altimeter callbacks use an explicit direct-degree interpretation. POS/OFS provide the background and bitmap pivots. Next-list links determine drawing order rather than spatial parenting. The two published comparison states differ by 831 pixels within (394,234)..(582,411), covering the selected gauges with an unchanged camera/geometry. This is stateless inspection, not a running FSX reference, temporal response, full instrument behavior or flight-mode support.</p>\n<p>The native Windows menu loaded the local aircraft catalog and live C172 exterior preview. On 18 October 2026, native capture and input verified camera movement, zoom, hiding the preview for the aircraft picker and replacing C172 with the selected A321 model (29,888 LOD-400 triangles). Visual resource loading does not require the single-piston flight model; this does not establish A321 flight support. Skinned parts, registration decals and automatic model animation remain incomplete. Aircraft chooser opening, FLY NOW launching the C172 runway renderer and Esc restoring the menu were checked. This establishes the launch path only; it does not establish FSX menu or simulation parity.</p>\n<p>The C172 ground path loads three wheel contacts and reports six omitted non-wheel contacts. Parking and braking scenarios pass, and explicit fixed inputs reach height above 30 m from the runway in 22.02 s. Native F4/B input also produced departure and visible AIRBORNE telemetry. This establishes a ground-to-air transition, not FSX takeoff fidelity or validated landing behavior.</p>\n<p>On 17 October 2026, the CFG inspector found <strong>117 aircraft variants with no read errors</strong> in the local FSX folder using Windows-1252. Variants are CFG sections, not unique aircraft models.</p>\n<p>The C172 exterior MDLX file has six LODs (5, 15, 35, 75, 150 and 400), nine texture references and 148 animated scene nodes. LOD 150 yields 11,314 solid triangles without skinned omissions. LOD 400 yields 13,527 triangles after omitting one skinned part. These counts establish geometry decoding, not correct animation or textured appearance.</p>\n<p>An offscreen C172 LOD 150 render was produced using the same wgpu pipeline as the native window, on an NVIDIA RTX 2080 through Vulkan. The Blue/Gold livery in texture.1 loads four of five referenced diffuse DDS files, including shared interior and pilot maps resolved through fallback. The missing DECAL_NNUMBER.DDS is reported; dynamic registration generation is not implemented. Visual inspection confirmed the blue/gold exterior mapping after preserving DDS row order and the original MDLX UV coordinates. Automatic animation state, interactive camera input and other backends are not established by this offscreen check. Manual native-key inspection is recorded separately in the <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">viewer guide</a>.</p>\n<p>The game installation and source resources are excluded from Git. Selected PNG engine screenshots are published in the website gallery at the project owner&#39;s request; intermediate captures remain under ignored target. CI uses original synthetic fixtures and does not contain or download FSX resources.</p>\n<p>The airborne prototype loads the local C172 variant, AIR and MDL without starting FSX. CFG payload and full usable Avgas yield 1,046.89 kg, a loaded CG and coupled inertia tensor. Reproducible checks hold 90 kt and 300 m for 60 seconds, verify initial control signs, report a full-power transient and enter the post-peak lift region during a power-off pull. Derived sea-level clean stall is 50.97 kt at this loading; the CFG lists 53.00 kt without an established loading condition. These are consistency checks, not validated flight performance. Native-window keyboard injection with this loading and the flight overlay verified short Right/Down impulses changing bank/pitch and vertical speed, plus P pause, R reset and Esc exit. Dedicated and NumLock-off navigation events are mapped consistently; sustained hardware holds remain unverified. Full details are in the <a href=\"#/docs/FLIGHT\">flight guide</a>.</p>\n<p>Visibility evidence: the local C172 has 61 conditions. Explicit prop-RPM-percent values 0, 8 and 20 each resolve 30 conditions and hide four LOD-150 draw batches, selecting the still, intermediate and blurred propeller alternatives. Identical-camera GPU readbacks differ only in the propeller region. These are explicit inspection inputs, not recorded FSX RPM or calibrated engine dynamics. The menu supplies an engine-stopped, stationary preview state and resolves all 61 C172 conditions; flight mode does not yet provide propeller or wheel RPM.</p>"
  },
  "FORMATS": {
    "source": "docs/FORMATS.md",
    "title": "Format notes",
    "html": "<h1>Format notes</h1>\n<h2>Evidence</h2>\n<p>This independent loader uses observed local data and the research published by FSDeveloper contributors: <a href=\"https://www.fsdeveloper.com/wiki/index.php/MDL_file_format_%28FSX%29\">FSX MDL format</a>. Their incomplete research is a starting point for experiments, not a full specification.</p>\n<p>No third-party importer implementation was copied. Tests create small original triangle files; original game assets stay in the local installation.</p>\n<h2>Visual model and eye-point references</h2>\n<p>The selected fltsim.N model suffix chooses model/model.suffix; its model.cfg provides [models] normal and optional interior. Both references are restricted to file-name components and receive .mdl when no extension is present. No embedded directory or absolute reference is accepted by this subset.</p>\n<p>An optional [views] eyepoint is exactly three finite feet values relative to reference_datum_position: longitudinal, lateral and vertical. The visual reader uses a zero datum if omitted; the full dynamics loader still requires its mass configuration. The sum converts to body forward/right/down SI coordinates, with vertical sign inversion. Eye components outside 1 km are rejected. Missing eye metadata leaves exterior viewing available. The installed C172 resolves to [-0.10668,-0.25908,-0.57912] body metres, or [-0.25908,0.57912,-0.10668] model-space metres before the aircraft/world transform. Camera-definition overlays and user camera profiles are not yet interpreted.</p>\n<h2>MDLX subset</h2>\n<p>MDLX model/view coordinates follow the left-handed Direct3D convention, with Y up and forward depth positive. The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDK/Environment%20Kit/Modeling%20SDK/Modeling%20File%20Formats.html\">modeling SDK coordinate-system notes</a> describe conversion from right-handed authoring coordinates. The projection uses target minus eye for forward and maps near/far to depth 0/1. The earlier right-handed camera mirrored geometry, cockpit text/control layout and the pilot&#39;s bank direction. This is corrected in the shared renderer; physics axes and simulation state are not changed. Synthetic projection tests verify that right remains screen-right, up remains up, depth is bounded, and a right-bank camera moves an eastern horizon point upward. Published GPU captures were rerendered; the older native menu-window image is labeled historical.</p>\n<p>Accepted files begin with RIFF, a little-endian payload length and the MDLX type. Nested chunks have a four-byte tag and a little-endian length. Observed MDLX chunks are packed without generic RIFF odd-byte padding; the C172&#39;s odd-length VISL chunk confirms this distinction.</p>\n<p>The reader checks parent bounds, record sizes, references, local triangle indices and finite geometry values. Files are limited to 256 MiB. Unknown chunks are skipped within validated bounds.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Chunk</th><th scope=\"col\">Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>MDLN</td><td>Friendly name</td></tr><tr><td>TEXT</td><td>64-byte texture name records</td></tr><tr><td>MATE</td><td>120-byte records; diffuse RGBA/map index, basic blend, alpha test and clamp mode</td></tr><tr><td>INDE</td><td>Little-endian u16 indices local to a part&#39;s vertex range</td></tr><tr><td>VERT</td><td>32-byte records; position, normal and UV; face normals recomputed for rendering</td></tr><tr><td>SKIN</td><td>Buffer reference marking unsupported skinned parts</td></tr><tr><td>TRAN</td><td>16 f32 static transform values</td></tr><tr><td>AMAP</td><td>Node type and TRAN reference</td></tr><tr><td>SCEN</td><td>Child, peer and AMAP byte offset; cycles rejected</td></tr><tr><td>LODE</td><td>Numeric LOD followed by PART chunks</td></tr><tr><td>PART</td><td>36-byte triangle-list part and validated buffer ranges</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>DirectX row-vector matrices are interpreted as transposed column-vector matrices with the same scalar storage. Child transforms compose with parents. Face normals are recomputed after transforming triangles, avoiding incorrect normal transforms under nonuniform scale.</p>\n<p>Animated nodes use static matrices by default; explicit manual clip selection evaluates rotation/translation keys. Skinned parts are omitted and counted. Triangle fans/strips, unreachable scene nodes and unsupported format generations are rejected explicitly.</p>\n<h2>DDS and texture lookup</h2>\n<p>The legacy DDS header and BC1/BC2/BC3 definitions follow Microsoft&#39;s <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3ddds/dx-graphics-dds-pguide\">DDS guide</a> and <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/direct3d10/d3d10-graphics-programming-guide-resources-block-compression\">block compression reference</a>. DXT1 selects its endpoint-order-dependent three/four-color palette. DXT3 always uses the four-color palette and explicit four-bit alpha. DXT5 uses the two endpoint-dependent alpha palettes and three-bit alpha selectors. Partial edge blocks are clipped to the declared dimensions.</p>\n<p>Uncompressed 32-bit RGB uses validated nonoverlapping contiguous channel masks and respects row pitch. Only the top mip is decoded. DDS rows and MDLX UVs are preserved without a vertical flip; this mapping was checked against the local C172 exterior. GPU diffuse textures use an sRGB format. This does not establish full FSX lighting or material parity.</p>\n<p>texture.cfg is read only for fltsim fallback entries, in numeric order. Relative Windows separators are normalized and canonical directory paths prevent cycles. Limits apply to file sizes, decoded pixels and directory traversal. No full content filesystem or external SimObjects resolver is claimed.</p>\n<h2>Next work</h2>\n<p>Tested bind-pose skinning, simulation-driven animation and visibility evaluation, material parity and generated registration textures. Back each interpretation with synthetic fixtures and local observations; do not silently reinterpret unknown layouts.</p>\n<h2>AIR subset</h2>\n<p>Legacy AIR files are sequences of a little-endian u32 identifier and u32 payload length followed by packed bytes. Record labels such as 1101 and 404 are hexadecimal: their numeric IDs are 0x1101 and 0x0404. Bounds, duplicate IDs and record counts are checked; input is limited to 4 MiB.</p>\n<p>Curve payloads contain a u32 point count followed by pairs of f64 angle/value entries. Angles must be finite and strictly increasing. The current reader uses linear interpolation and clamps outside the endpoint domain. It loads lift versus body AoA from 0x0404 and pitch moment versus AoA from 0x0473, plus selected signed integer and double coefficients from the 250-byte 0x1101 layout. Integer aerodynamic derivatives use the documented 1/2048 scale. Modern override records are rejected until their precedence is implemented.</p>\n<p>The independent reader was checked against field definitions distributed with AirEd and the local C172 data. Reference: <a href=\"https://www.aero.sors.fr/fsairfile.html\">aircraft dynamics resources</a>. Source definitions and game data are not included. The <a href=\"#/docs/FLIGHT\">flight guide</a> documents coordinate signs, units, approximations and numerical evidence; decoding these records does not establish complete FSX flight-model behavior.</p>\n<h2>MDLX animation-key subset</h2>\n<p>ANIB in the observed C172 contains an inner packed RIFF with XANH and XANL, followed by zero padding. XANL holds XANI groups: GUID bytes, float length, a 16-byte type field and a terminated parameter string, then packed children. XAPS contains terminated variable/units/scale/bias/optional-lag fields. XAPC contains a terminated expression with an optional #lag# prefix. A bounded, numeric RPN subset evaluates supported A-variable expressions into authored frame positions; results clamp to the clip length. Static inspection stays read-only. Temporal animation can additionally read/write caller-owned L: data and use s0..s49, sp0..sp49 and l0..l49 scratch registers. Unknown syntax, missing values and invalid results retain diagnostics. The temporal PoseState API limits movement to Lag keyframes per simulation second for both XAPS and XAPC. Its first available target initializes immediately; unavailable targets hold the previous known pose and retain the failure diagnostic. Recreate state on model changes or simulation reset. This initialization/missing-value policy is explicit and has not been compared with FSX. Static plan_pose inspection still defers positive lag because one sample has no time history. XAPC source is limited to 16 KiB, each program to 4096 instructions and the ANIB total to 262144 compiled instructions. Duplicate XAPS/XAPC drivers are rejected. XANS begins with channel type, transform ID and float track length. Observed channels 0 and 2 contain XANK keys with a one-byte kind, float frame and respectively XYZW quaternion (kind 4) or XYZ translation (kind 3). SGAL is one signed 16-bit transform ID per scene node, with -1 for no linkage. Multiple groups can reference the same transform ID.</p>\n<p>The reader validates parent lengths, zero padding, terminated strings, finite values, increasing key times within track length, nonzero quaternions, duplicate channels and SGAL references. Limits apply to clips, tracks and keys. Other channel layouts fail explicitly. Keys clamp at their endpoints; interior translation is linear and rotation uses normalized shortest-arc slerp. The pose API composes selected clip deltas in file order after static local matrices, then traverses parents before children. Arbitrary multi-clip composition has not been compared with FSX.</p>\n<p>The binary interpretation was checked against local bytes and original synthetic fixtures. The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/modeling/using_modeling_tools.html\">Prepar3D modeling SDK</a> explains variable scaling/bias and lag semantics; reading those fields does not establish an implemented SimVar or gauge system. See the <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">viewer guide</a> for supported inspection and its evidence.</p>\n<p>Model-local contexts are limited to 512 named finite numeric values. Names and unit strings are case-insensitive ASCII, bounded to 256 and 64 characters. First successful references initialize to zero. A variable retains its first unit string; mismatched-unit access is unavailable rather than silently converting. Registers start uninitialized on each evaluation; reads before a store fail explicitly. Stores sN retain the stack value; spN and &gt;L: consume it. Exactly one finite result is required. Failed expressions, invalid animation targets and failed temporal steps do not commit local writes.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://prepar3d.com/SDKv6/sdk/references/variables/local_variables.html\">SDK local-variable description</a> describes shared L: scope. The current context is shared only between a visual instance&#39;s Sim clips with tracks, in file order, and is cleared on simulation reset. Shared XML-gauge/panel scope, cross-model lifetime and full unit conversion remain unimplemented. The <a href=\"https://docs.flightsimulator.com/html/Additional_Information/Reverse_Polish_Notation.htm\">Microsoft RPN operator reference</a> documents register stack behavior; this is not a complete FSX execution contract. No A: writes, events, native calls, gauge DLLs, loops or strings are supported.</p>\n<h2>Panel placement subset</h2>\n<p><code>fsx-vehicle::panel</code> reads the selected variant&#39;s panel.cfg without loading gauge code. Aircraft definitions select panel/panel.cfg or panel.SUFFIX/panel.cfg; suffixes must be filename components. The <code>inspect-panel</code> command selects variant 0, reports all configured panels and checks each dynamic texture in the native interior against the virtual-cockpit configuration.</p>\n<p>WindowNN and VCockpitNN sections sort numerically. Gauge references retain the module and instrument names, X/Y panel coordinates, optional positive width and height, and the remaining parameter string including commas. Omitted dimensions remain unresolved until the gauge&#39;s own intrinsic size is available. The loader visits indices below 100 in numeric order and then contiguous indices from 100; later entries after a gap are reported separately. This follows the documented ordering in the <a href=\"https://prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/simobjects/panels_and_gauges/panel_configuration_files.html\">panel configuration reference</a>, but native FSX ordering at gaps has not been measured.</p>\n<p>Panel coordinate extents, explicit pixel extents and RGB background colors are read separately; no automatic scaling or aspect-ratio resolution is applied. Virtual-cockpit texture names start with $, are at most 15 bytes and match the model case-insensitively. Duplicate numeric panel/gauge aliases and duplicate VC texture names fail explicitly. Limits are 2 MiB of decoded CFG text, 256 panels, 4096 loaded gauges, indices below 4096 and finite coordinates within +/-65536. Other section/directive names are retained as unsupported diagnostics.</p>\n<p>Background bitmaps, window positioning/visibility, panel aliases, render-to-texture views, XML/CAB gauges, native DLL/GAU callbacks and panel interaction are not yet implemented. The parser does not execute or extract installed gauge code. Tests use original synthetic declarations; installed panel configurations remain local.</p>\n<h2>Windows bitmap subset</h2>\n<p><code>Image::dib</code> reads a packed native RT_BITMAP payload; <code>Image::bmp</code> additionally reads the 14-byte file header and explicit pixel offset. Supported data uses a 40-byte BITMAPINFOHEADER, one plane, BI_RGB and 4/8/24 bits per pixel. Indexed images use BGR-reserved palette entries, with biClrUsed or the full bit-depth palette when zero. Reserved bytes remain opaque rather than becoming alpha. Four-bit pixels use the high nibble first; unused nibbles and DWORD row padding are skipped. Positive heights read bottom-up, negative heights top-down.</p>\n<p>Dimensions are bounded to 4096, files to 256 MiB and palettes to 256 entries. Missing palette indices, overlapping BMP offsets, truncated rows and invalid declared extents fail. BMP file trailers outside bfSize are ignored. BI_RGB biSizeImage may be zero; otherwise it must cover the required rows and fit in the payload. The separate <code>Image::bmp_alpha</code> mode requires 32-bit BI_RGB file pixels and preserves their explicit BGRA alpha, including fully transparent nonblack pixels. It is selected only by the gauge&#39;s Alpha property; reserved palette bytes and ordinary DIB high bytes are not guessed to be opacity. RLE, bitfields, 1/16-bit pixels, extended headers, native DIB alpha and color profiles remain unsupported. The <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wingdi/ns-wingdi-bitmapinfoheader\">Windows bitmap reference</a> documents these structures; the implementation uses no GDI calls.</p>\n<p><code>fsx-render::panel::static_roots</code> is an explicit artwork-inspection compositor. It requires VC panel and gauge dimensions, exact resource language, zero-origin static roots and supported flags. It maps CFG rectangles into panel pixels, clips without stretching, samples source pixels by nearest neighbor and applies RGB-black transparency when root image flag bit 0 is set. Mask, alpha, hidden, no-stretch and no-translation roots are diagnosed instead of guessed. Other elements are counted but omitted. Native update callbacks, conditional visibility, day/night/luminous blending and FSX sampling/placement parity are unimplemented. The result can be bound to one unambiguous model texture slot before GPU upload; dimension, RGBA length and total appearance memory budgets are checked.</p>\n<h2>Native gauge metadata subset</h2>\n<p><code>fsx-vehicle::gauge</code> treats a native DLL/GAU file as bytes. It resolves the PE32 Linkage export and preferred-image pointers to file-backed data; it never maps the library into the process, applies relocations, resolves imports or calls code. Only x86 PE32 images and the shared header prefix for gauge versions 800, 900 and 1000 are supported. Other versions and PE32+ fail explicitly.</p>\n<p>Each header references one root-pointer variable. An element&#39;s next-element field references a null-terminated child-pointer array. The reader preserves authored child order and records type, resource ID, position and image flags; cycles fail, while shared elements are indexed once per gauge. Repeated Linkage entries pointing to the same header are retained. Name lookup is ASCII case-insensitive and rejects ambiguous names pointing to distinct headers. Null encoded roots remain empty rather than fabricating runtime-generated data.</p>\n<p>PE resources retain type/name/language identity, including UTF-16 named keys and code-page metadata. Resource access returns the exact indexed file slice without automatic locale fallback or writing extracted files. Type 2 identifies bitmap resources, whose payloads are DIB data rather than complete .bmp files. Bitmap decoding is implemented for the subset above. The separate static root preview does not implement moving element transforms, callbacks, failure systems, native variable access or operational gauge drawing.</p>\n<p>Limits are 64 MiB per file, 96 non-overlapping sections, 8192 export entries, 512 gauge headers, 2048 distinct elements and 8192 graph edges per gauge, 65536 elements per library, 8192 resource leaves and 32768 resource-directory entries. Name limits are 256 bytes for gauge/export names and 256 UTF-16 units for resource keys. Truncated/overlapping ranges, unbacked virtual zero-fill, forwarded Linkage exports, malformed ordinals, graph cycles and shared/overdeep resource directories fail explicitly. Windows-1252 gauge-name decoding requires the explicit option.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/debug/pe-format\">Microsoft PE specification</a> documents export/resource structures. The installed FSX header declarations and the <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.org/SDKv4/sdk/panels_and_user_interface/gauges/references/gauges.html\">gauge API reference</a> describe the gauge metadata layout. These facts do not establish native ABI or rendering compatibility. Tests construct original non-executable PE containers; no installed DLL, header, sample source or bitmap is included.</p>\n<p>Reproduce the local C172 configuration/library cross-check without running FSX:</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -- inspect-gauges &quot;./FSX/Gauges/Cessna172.dll&quot; --windows-1252\ncargo run --locked -p fsx-check --example native-panel-check -- &quot;./FSX&quot;</code></pre>\n<p>The example uses the explicit installation&#39;s stock C172 and Gauges folder. It is not a general add-on module search-order implementation.</p>\n<h2>Native needle metadata and inspection transforms</h2>\n<p>The content reader records each needle&#39;s source token, callback RVA, encoded max-degrees-per-tick field and nonlinearity table. POS in the common element header is the pivot in the gauge background; OFS is the pivot inside the needle bitmap. Runtime reference fields are separate and are not substituted for OFS. Next-list links establish drawing order, not a hierarchy of spatial transforms.</p>\n<p>Tables contain 24-byte entries with signed X/Y points and an f64 value; the runtime degree-cache member is ignored. Limits are 512 points per table, 65536 points per library, coordinates within +/-65536 and finite table values within 1e12 in magnitude. Invalid pointers, inconsistent pointer/count pairs and unsupported extents fail explicitly. Reading-to-angle inspection requires at least two strictly increasing or decreasing values, unwraps the authored clockwise point order across +/-pi, interpolates angles and clamps outside the table&#39;s endpoint values. Angles use screen coordinates (+Y down).</p>\n<p>The renderer rotates the original bitmap about OFS, places it about POS and scales it into the explicit CFG rectangle using the background image extent. Clipping preserves the transform; nearest-neighbor sampling and RGB-black keying match the other artwork inspection path. Hidden-tree predecessors and unsupported masks/alpha/transform flags are rejected. A fresh root preview is composed for each state; repeated paints are not an erase/update lifecycle.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv6/sdk/panels_and_user_interface/gauges/references/gauges_api_overview.html\">gauge API reference</a> describes needle pivots, next-list order and clockwise nonlinearity points. The implementation is an inspection subset: table clamping, sampling, temporal lag, failures and the full FSX panel rasterization contract remain unverified. Table-free result interpretation must be explicit; it is not inferred for arbitrary callbacks. The checked example opts into degrees only for the three Cessna altimeter functions. Other native layers and live token binding remain unimplemented, and no original artwork or SDK implementation is bundled.</p>\n<h2>Isolated native update-call protocol</h2>\n<p>The separate tools/fsx-gauge-host process can execute a selected installed needle update function on Windows x86. It is outside the Rust content-reader and engine dependency graph. Build and check without running FSX:</p>\n<pre><code>./scripts/build-gauge-host.ps1\n./target/fsx-gauge-host.exe --probe ./FSX Cessna172.dll Airspeed\n./target/fsx-gauge-host.exe --sample ./FSX Cessna172.dll Airspeed 1001 75\n./scripts/check-native-gauges.ps1 -FsxRoot ./FSX -Output target/native-gauge-check.ndjson</code></pre>\n<p>Probe mode loads the selected DLL but invokes no exported module/element functions. Sample mode calls ModuleInit/ModuleDeinit around one selected needle update call. It fills current and previous numeric source values with the same explicit raw input. No simulator token lookup, units or native PANELS services are supplied. Schema-1 single-line JSON identifies source native-gauge-probe, mode, pointer_bits, library/gauge, lifecycle calls, needles and optional sample resource/token/raw_input/callback_result. All numeric results must be finite.</p>\n<p>Only the five checked stock library filenames under the explicit installation&#39;s canonical Gauges folder are accepted. Names are bounded ASCII; pointers remain within the loaded image and are checked for page access. Limits are 64 MiB on disk, 512 headers, 2048 elements, 8192 edges and raw magnitudes up to 1e12. Structured exceptions become errors; a five-second watchdog terminates only the helper. Errors use stderr and a nonzero exit status. No native resources or SDK headers are copied into the repository.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/libloaderapi/nf-libloaderapi-loadlibraryexw\">Windows library-loader reference</a> describes native loading, and the gauge API documents the four-byte x86 slot layout and stdcall element callbacks. The helper does not implement a full query/install/initialize/update/generate/draw/kill sequence, panel callbacks, shared variables, events or rendering. Its raw return values need the element&#39;s authored mapping before they can drive a renderer. No instrument/FSX parity is claimed by an isolated successful function call.</p>\n<h2>CAB and ACE XML gauge documents</h2>\n<p>The read-only vehicle layer accepts single-cabinet version 1.3 archives with stored or MSZIP folders. Compressed input and total decoded folder bytes are each bounded to 64 MiB; file/folder tables to 8,192 entries. MSZIP resets its DEFLATE state per block while preserving the preceding folder dictionary. Declared block sizes, optional checksums, metadata/data ranges and overlapping folders are checked. Header/folder/data reserve areas are supported. Offsets are limited to the declared cabinet prefix; appended bytes are not interpreted or trusted as a certificate. Chained/split cabinets, Quantum and LZX fail explicitly. Names use normalized relative keys with ambiguous/case-colliding names rejected; non-UTF-8 names require explicit Windows-1252 unless marked UTF-8 by CAB metadata. These boundaries follow the <a href=\"https://download.microsoft.com/download/4/D/A/4DA14F27-B4EF-4170-A6E6-5B1EF85B1BAA/%5BMS-CAB%5D.pdf\">Microsoft cabinet format</a> and <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/exchange_server_protocols/ms-mci/27f0a9bf-9567-4e40-ad66-6ae9ab9d2786\">MSZIP specification</a>.</p>\n<p>ACE XML parsing is bounded to 1 MiB, 8,192 nodes and depth 64. It retains attributes, text and unknown nodes, resolves predefined/numeric entities and requires an AceXML document with one SimGauge.Gauge. DTDs, external entities, processing instructions and unsupported encodings fail. Image Name references resolve within the XML member&#39;s directory; ArtDirectory never opens a path on the host. Structural decoding alone does not make visual elements functional. The typed text-gauge reader separately accepts a static background plus translated GaugeText, parented Element Visibility, dimensions, optional FontFace, FontHeight, alignment, transparent text and default/alternate COLORREF colors (0xBBGGRR). Unsupported visual properties and duplicate scalar properties fail explicitly. A small named-color subset and hexadecimal values are accepted. Root translation, rotation, bold/weight, alternate fonts, opaque text boxes and scripted visual properties are not applied.</p>\n<p>The renderer requires an explicit local font file (bounded to 16 MiB), validates an authored FontFace against the selected full face name, and reports when that file substitutes for an absent FontFace. It bounds glyph counts/metrics and uses single-line ASCII, fontdue coverage and pixels-per-em FontHeight. Text clips to its authored rectangle; composed RGBA gauges scale/clip into panel coordinates using straight-alpha blending. Fonts and installed images remain external. Default FSX font selection, complex shaping, GDI height/kerning/rasterization and night-art selection remain unverified or unsupported. See the <a href=\"https://docs.rs/fontdue/0.9.4/fontdue/struct.Font.html\">fontdue metrics reference</a> and the viewer&#39;s explicit-font inspection commands.</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -- inspect-cabinet &quot;./FSX/Gauges/n_number_plaque.cab&quot;\n# Optional independent byte comparison; expanded files remain under ignored target/.\nNew-Item -ItemType Directory -Force target/cabinet-reference | Out-Null\nexpand.exe FSX/Gauges/n_number_plaque.cab &#39;-F:*&#39; target/cabinet-reference\ncargo run -p fsx-vehicle --example cabinet-verify -- FSX/Gauges/n_number_plaque.cab target/cabinet-reference</code></pre>\n<p>Locally the XML and both C172 plaque BMP members (6,353 decoded bytes) matched Windows Expand exactly. Tests use independent synthetic archives, scripts and image names, including a second DEFLATE block that requires the first block&#39;s dictionary. No installed XML, bitmap or compressed original member is committed.</p>\n<p>The typed background reader also accepts explicit Image Alpha=True, selecting the separate 32-bit BI_RGB BGRA decoder. Ordinary BMP/DIB decoding never guesses opacity from a reserved byte. Partial coverage reaches panel composition as straight alpha. Alpha plus black-key Transparent=True is rejected while its precedence is unverified. Luminous is not accepted as Alpha: the SDK describes inverse-alpha illumination with panel [Color] values, which is a separate lighting contract still unimplemented. See the <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv2/LearningCenter/simobjects/panels_and_gauges/creating_xml_gauges.html\">Image property reference</a>.</p>\n<p>PanelConfig retains optional [Color] Day, Night and Luminous values as RGB byte triplets, without inventing defaults for absent keys. Unknown color directives and the unimplemented application remain diagnostic. Parsing these values does not dim/tint existing aircraft images and does not imply automatic _night.bmp selection. Such selection and global illumination need separate evidence.</p>\n<h2>GaugeString evaluation subset</h2>\n<p>The independent script layer compiles literal UTF-8 text, a single read-only A: string lookup followed by a bounded s format, numeric A:/P: output and conditions followed by %{if}/%{else}/%{end}, percent escaping, standard newline/tab/return/backslash escapes and the observed default/alternate color commands. Values are emitted as colored text spans, not reparsed markup. Numeric lookups receive separate namespace/name/units; indexed A: names cannot alias P: names. Unknown or non-finite values fail, and inactive branches do not resolve their variables.</p>\n<p>Source is bounded to 16 KiB, 2,048 string parts, nesting 32 and 8 KiB of output. Numeric subexpressions retain the existing RPN stack/instruction limits. The ordinary model RPN compiler still rejects P: reads; GaugeString does not enable model-local writes, events, shared panel variables or host callbacks. General string arithmetic, loops, case, alternate fonts and other escape codes remain unsupported. Literal whitespace is preserved; full FSX indentation/wrapping rules and rendered text metrics remain unverified. The conditional/format syntax is described in the <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv2/LearningCenter/simobjects/panels_and_gauges/creating_xml_gauges.html\">XML gauge SDK reference</a>; the clr2/default mapping is checked against the installed plaque&#39;s expression, not a live FSX pixel comparison.</p>\n<p>Numeric d output supports rounding, width, sign for positive nonzero results, leading zeros/spaces and left alignment. Negative zero is normalized for d. Fixed f output uses requested fractional precision, ignoring width/zero padding as described in the SDK. Width is limited to 256, precision to 16. Integer conversions are limited to the exact f64 integer range. The s format accepts width-formatted A: strings and whole numeric values; fractional numeric-to-string conversion fails. String width counts characters rather than UTF-8 bytes and never reparses returned data as markup.</p>\n<p>Integer halves round away from zero; fixed formatting uses Rust&#39;s binary-f64 formatter, including ties-to-even behavior. These edge policies require an FSX reference. Bare f currently uses six fractional digits, an inference from the <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cpp/c-runtime-library/format-specification-syntax-printf-and-wprintf-functions\">Microsoft C formatting default</a>, not proof of the gauge runtime&#39;s choice. Exponential/hex formats, star widths, precision on d/s, and floating sign/alignment flags fail explicitly.</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --features viewer --example xml-numeric-render-check -- FSX/Gauges/n_number_plaque.cab n_number_plaque_background.bmp C:/Windows/Fonts/arial.ttf target/xml-numeric-check</code></pre>\n<p>This command renders independently authored synthetic COM/altitude/VSI/heading text using an explicitly selected installed background/font. Five states check powered/hidden/restored text with synthetic electrical/avionics input values. It does not add operational radio, engine or flight-variable bindings to the aircraft.</p>\n<h2>XML element visibility subset</h2>\n<p>Element Visibility accepts a plain, bounded numeric RPN source. Parent/child conditions remain separate nodes, with at most 8,192 nodes and 1 MiB total condition source in the renderer. Each node is compiled before evaluation; parents must precede children. Expressions use the shared GaugeExpression A:/P: read-only resolver, preserving namespace/name/units and model-mode isolation. Nonzero finite values show the element; zero hides it. A hidden parent hides all descendants and skips their lookups, while a parent&#39;s result is shared across its text children. Active unresolved values, malformed graphs or non-finite results return an error. Hidden text emits an empty span list and no glyphs; its GaugeString syntax is still checked. Authored ordering between a parent&#39;s GaugeText and nested Elements is preserved.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv2/LearningCenter/simobjects/panels_and_gauges/creating_xml_gauges.html\">Element SDK reference</a> defines Visibility as an optional numeric script. This subset does not implement Lua, macros, shared locals, hidden update events or native lifecycle services. Synthetic visibility/read-count/pixel tests are not an original-FSX electrical or hidden-update comparison.</p>\n<p>Aircraft/VisualAircraft expose optional selected-variant atc_id (at most 256 bytes, without control characters), preserving explicit emptiness and absence. This is authored configuration, not a user-edited flight/save-game registration or a live SimConnect reading. The integration checker below requires an explicit expected ID and six synthetic time-of-day/light cases; it writes no aircraft configuration and does not launch FSX.</p>\n<pre><code>cargo run -p fsx-app --example xml-gauge-check -- FSX/Gauges/n_number_plaque.cab n_number_plaque.xml FSX/SimObjects/Airplanes/C172 0 G-BAFM</code></pre>\n<h2>Native string element subset</h2>\n<p>The x86, four-byte-packed ELEMENT_STRING record is 324 bytes. Authored token slots begin at 60/88/116, source callback pointers at 168/172/176, image extent at 192/196, COLORREFs at 208/212/216 and a 32-byte face name at 248. Weight, charset and DrawText flags occupy 280/284/288, maximum string length is u16 at 292, selection-sequence pointer is at 300, font height at 308 and update callback at 312. Callbacks and the first sequence record are bounded within the PE image; the selection sequence is not traversed. Face names require a terminator and explicit encoding. Zero font height remains authored automatic sizing.</p>\n<p>The three MODULE_VAR values are unions. The separate native string probe requires f64 or u32 payload interpretation for every slot; it does not resolve token types, BCD units, source callbacks or live simulator state. Its guarded ASCII output and explicit lifecycle checks are described in the helper README. Successful text samples are not native font/GDI or instrument-parity validation. The <a href=\"https://prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/simobjects/sample_code/gauges.html\">published gauge header</a> and <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv2/LearningCenter/simobjects/panels_and_gauges/programming_c_gauges.html\">C-gauge API reference</a> describe these structures and callbacks. Implementation/tests use independent declarations; no installed header, DLL, font or native sample source is committed.</p>\n<h2>Reference NDJSON</h2>\n<p>The separate x86 recorder keeps schema 1 for title/elapsed time and 12 control readings. Optional schema 2 adds unsigned 32-bit <code>object_id</code>/<code>sample_index</code> and 32 named instrument/flight-state readings under <code>instruments</code>, all in explicitly requested units. A single title-plus-44-double SimConnect definition produces a 648-byte packet. Names containing DEGREES do not imply the default API unit; the recorder requests degrees, feet, knots and feet per minute explicitly.</p>\n<p>Readers bound files to 64 MiB, rows to 16 KiB and recordings to 100,000 samples. Schema 2 requires every known field, finite values within magnitude 1e12, valid failure enums/bools, and nonnegative simulator time/rate. Sequential replay requires contiguous indices, monotonic elapsed wall time and consistent source, aircraft title and user-object ID. Simulator time can pause or rewind. An absolute clock rewind starts a new native session even when normalized time was already zero. Metadata labels are not proof of a capture&#39;s authenticity.</p>\n<p>The native replay adapter consumes measured indicated airspeed/altitude, VSI and gyro indications directly. It does not substitute true speed, MSL altitude, body attitude or ideal ISA values. It explicitly reports/rejects instrument states outside the current healthy, neutral native profile. Available stateless model bindings also consume recorded variables; unsupported/lagged groups stay omitted. ISA/weather and body/gyro differences are diagnostic output without a parity verdict. The extended request still needs live original-FSX acquisition checks.</p>\n<p>See <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">the viewer guide</a> for capture, validator and replay commands.</p>\n<h2>MDLX visibility subset</h2>\n<p>VISL contains packed VISS/VINS/VISC/VINC string chunks, bounded to 16 KiB per condition. Strings must terminate and may have only zero bytes afterward. SGVL is one signed i16 condition index per scene node, with -1 for no condition; counts and references are checked. Direct Sim reads currently accept only the observed variable#units form. Code may have a #lag# prefix; positive lag is reported unsupported rather than silently ignored. VISS/VISC default visible; VINS/VINC default hidden when evaluation cannot be completed. A nonzero result selects visible. Parent visibility propagates through the scene traversal.</p>\n<p>The C172 byte layout was inspected independently; the <a href=\"https://www.fsdeveloper.com/wiki/index.php/MDL_file_format_%28FSX%29\">FSDeveloper MDLX notes</a> identify these chunks. The <a href=\"https://www.prepar3d.com/SDKv3/LearningCenter/modeling/using_modeling_tools.html\">modeling SDK</a> describes visibility parameters, and the <a href=\"https://docs.flightsimulator.com/html/Additional_Information/Reverse_Polish_Notation.htm\">Microsoft RPN reference</a> describes stack/branch syntax. These documents do not establish complete FSX runtime equivalence. Tests use original synthetic expressions and triangle data; no installed model expressions or SDK implementation are distributed.</p>"
  },
  "PORTING": {
    "source": "docs/PORTING.md",
    "title": "Porting checkpoint",
    "html": "<h1>Porting checkpoint</h1>\n<p>The target remains FSX 1:1 across interface, flight behavior, content and APIs. This is a long-term acceptance target; no subsystem claims complete parity.</p>\n<h2>Current stage — 20 October 2026</h2>\n<p>After <code>b60cad2</code>, the read-only PE decoder recognizes x86 ELEMENT_STRING metadata: three tokens/source callbacks, update callback, authored dimensions, font/weight/charset/height, text flags, maximum length, COLORREFs and a bounded selection-sequence pointer. Runtime string/GDI/source-value pointers are not followed. All five checked libraries decoded; 20 reported string entries include aliases. The C172 clock declares Quartz, automatic font height and eight chars.</p>\n<p>The x86 helper adds <code>--sample-string</code> with explicit f64/u32 union payloads, ModuleInit/Deinit, executable-pointer checks, source-callback rejection and a guarded output buffer sized to the authored maximum (1..256). Its synthetic self-test contains a deliberate overrun and detects a prefix overwrite; the build/CI self-test gate now requires the string guard result.</p>\n<p><code>native-string-check</code> resolves the Nav-Comm 1 string from tokens [23,617,0], not a hardcoded RVA, and validates three native outputs from synthetic BCD words: 118.25/120.50, 122.50/127.00 and 119.85/124.30. Metadata, type/value echoes, text, guards and lifecycle fields pass (<code>target/native-string-check-01/</code>). Numeric n-slot injection alone is incorrect for these BCD union fields; input interpretation is now mandatory rather than inferred. Radio state/band logic and native text rendering remain unimplemented.</p>\n<p>The isolated C172/C182 clock callbacks returned blank text; a secondary clock and transponder sample faulted inside the callback, contained by the helper. No operational clock or complete gauge lifecycle is claimed, and private gauge state/services were not fabricated to force a result. Next work should establish the required lifecycle and native font sizing before integrating these strings.</p>\n<p>146 all-feature Rust test executions pass, plus the enhanced x86 self-test and three installed string samples. The existing 121-frame native flight check with the XML plaque still passes pause/reset/view/ground and helper-close checks (<code>target/native-string-flight-regression-01/</code>). Original-FSX acquisition remains deferred; the broad 1:1 goal remains active.</p>\n<h3>Previous completed XML visibility stage</h3>\n<p>After <code>93233bb</code>, translated XML text elements accept bounded read-only Visibility expressions. Decoding retains parent-before-child condition nodes and authored text/child drawing order. Rendering evaluates each condition once per state; a hidden ancestor skips descendant lookups and text evaluation. Invalid, unavailable or non-finite active conditions fail explicitly. String syntax is compiled even for hidden text, so unsupported markup is not silently accepted.</p>\n<p>The numeric raster checker now covers five synthetic states: two powered baselines, an avionics-hidden row, all rows hidden by the parent, and restored power. Hidden providers intentionally withhold unused values, and the report records read names/visibility. Restoring power restores identical pixels; the two original powered baselines remain byte-identical. Images were inspected under <code>target/xml-visibility-render-01/</code>. These conditions use synthetic power inputs and do not implement the aircraft&#39;s electrical system.</p>\n<p>145 all-feature workspace test executions pass, including shared A:/P: numeric expression boundaries, parent/child graph checks, lazy lookups and drawing-order coverage. Actual FSX hidden-gauge update/lifecycle semantics and visibility side effects remain unverified; writes/shared locals/events are unsupported. Original-FSX acquisition remains deferred and the full 1:1 goal stays active.</p>\n<h3>Previous completed explicit-alpha stage</h3>\n<p>After <code>2f5045b</code>, XML Image Alpha=True selects explicit 32-bit BI_RGB BGRA decoding. Ordinary BMP/DIB decoding does not reinterpret unused high bytes as opacity. Zero/partial/full alpha survive CAB -&gt; typed XML -&gt; image -&gt; authored panel composition, checked using independent synthetic resources and exact expected pixels. Combined Alpha/black-key transparency fails until validated; Luminous remains distinct and unsupported rather than becoming opacity.</p>\n<p>PanelConfig now retains optional authored [Color] Day/Night/Luminous RGB values, with byte-range checks and diagnostics for unimplemented lighting application. The local C172 inspector reports the installed values directly. These metadata are prerequisites for lighting, not a lighting model. The SDK documents an inverse-alpha luminous mask and panel colors; it does not establish automatic _night.bmp selection for this loader. No suffix substitution was guessed.</p>\n<p>142 all-feature workspace test executions pass, including four new cases for BGRA/truncation/orientation, typed Alpha behavior, exact composition and authored color metadata. Actual FSX night selection, blending/dimming, Bright/Luminous behavior and time-of-day/panel-light services still need implementation and reference evidence. The current flight plaque stays in its daylight profile. The existing two numeric/daytime raster cases also remained byte-identical after the alpha change (<code>target/xml-alpha-day-regression-01/</code>).</p>\n<h3>Previous completed numeric-text stage</h3>\n<p>After <code>6d0a1c9</code>, GaugeString additionally supports bounded numeric d/f output and width-formatted A: strings. Numeric expressions retain explicit A:/P: namespace and units. Integer output rounds, preserves sign and supports space/ zero padding and left alignment; fixed output uses explicit fractional precision and the SDK&#39;s width-ignored behavior. Limits are width 256, precision 16 and the existing 8 KiB total text budget. Unknown formats and unsafe widths fail.</p>\n<p>The synthetic <code>xml-numeric-render-check</code> passes two complete XML -&gt; lookup -&gt; format -&gt; font rasterization cases for COM/altitude/VSI/heading text. Changing inputs changed 2,824 pixels in the 400x68 diagnostic image; both local images were inspected (<code>target/xml-numeric-render-01/</code>). These are synthetic diagnostic readings, not operational radios or extra telemetry in the flight cockpit. 138 all-feature workspace test executions pass, including four new formatting tests and the existing registration/conditional/renderer checks.</p>\n<p>Integer half ties currently round away from zero; fixed formatting follows Rust&#39;s binary-f64 rounding. Bare f uses a six-digit precision policy inferred from C formatting, without proof that FSX uses that default. These choices, zero-sign behavior and fractional numeric-to-string conversion still need original-FSX validation. The latter currently fails explicitly. Original-FSX acquisition remains deferred; full compatibility is not claimed.</p>\n<h3>Previous completed XML rasterization stage</h3>\n<p>After <code>139f1bf</code>, the C172 XML registration plaque can be rasterized and composed into its authored VC placement. The vehicle layer accepts a checked static background/translated GaugeText subset and rejects unknown visual properties. The renderer uses a caller-selected local font, bounded single-line ASCII, COLORREF colors, alignment, glyph coverage, clipping and straight-alpha blending. Neither original fonts nor artwork are added to the repository.</p>\n<p>The viewer&#39;s opt-in <code>--xml-gauge-font &lt;font.ttf&gt;</code> requires <code>--native-gauges</code> and adds the authored variant registration to the current daylight flight profile. The installed XML omits FontFace: the caller explicitly supplies that mapping. FontHeight is interpreted as pixels per em; the original FSX default face, GDI metrics/filtering and rasterization parity remain unverified. Panel lights, night-background selection, broader text formatting and interaction are not implemented by this option. Original-FSX acquisition remains deferred.</p>\n<p><code>xml-text-render-check</code> rendered four registration/color cases from the installed XML using the explicitly selected Windows Arial file. Variant 0&#39;s alternate color changed 276 cockpit pixels within (423,200)..(466,209); both variants and their 89x25 gauge images were rendered. Local images were inspected. Reports and ignored images are under <code>target/xml-text-render-02/</code> (initial inspection also under <code>target/xml-text-render-01/</code>). This is a synthetic color-state comparison, using the authored daytime background in both states, not a night-lighting model.</p>\n<p>The actual viewer CLI snapshot passed with the new flag. The 121-frame native flight regression with the XML plaque passed ten paused reuses, reset/helper restart, view round trips and ground checks; reset/view pixels stayed identical (<code>target/xml-text-flight-02/</code>). 134 all-feature workspace test executions pass, including XML property rejection, color order, alpha and placement clipping.</p>\n<h2>Previous stage — 19 October 2026</h2>\n<p>After <code>cb787c4</code>, GaugeString now evaluates bounded literal/string output, read-only A:/P: conditions, nested if/else/end and default/alternate color spans. Selected aircraft variants retain their authored atc_id, including an explicitly empty value; missing IDs are not invented from titles. Writes, local/shared gauge state, loops and unsupported formats fail explicitly.</p>\n<p><code>xml-gauge-check</code> evaluates the installed plaque&#39;s original XML with the actual variant registration and explicitly synthetic time-of-day/panel-light inputs. Variants 0/1/2/5 passed all six combinations each: 24 rows under <code>target/xml-gauge-check-01/variants.ndjson</code>, with eight alternate-color results. The interpreter never reparses returned registration text as executable markup. 130 workspace test executions with all features pass, including four new synthetic string tests and expanded variant-selection/bounds checks.</p>\n<p>At that checkpoint the plaque was still omitted from flight rendering. The next stage needed typed GaugeText visual properties, an explicit font source/default-font decision, text rasterization and VC placement with day/night/registration image checks. No font metrics or real-FSX text-rasterization parity is established by the successful expression evaluation. Original-FSX acquisition remains deferred.</p>\n<h3>Previous completed CAB/XML decoding stage</h3>\n<p>Current HEAD before this stage: <code>3cf4387</code>. The goal remains active. The user deferred original-FSX reference acquisition and asked to continue independently. The attempted local FSX launch was canceled at Windows consent; no live capture was obtained. Only ignored scenario/config copies were prepared, and the source user configuration SHA-256 was unchanged (<code>target/reference-fsx-live-01/</code>).</p>\n<p>The next XML-panel stage now has a bounded stored/MSZIP CAB reader and a read-only ACE XML tree decoder in fsx-vehicle. It handles reserved archive fields, cross-block DEFLATE history and contained image references. The CLI command <code>inspect-cabinet</code> inspects these documents; ArtDirectory is never followed. Unknown nodes remain visible in the tree rather than becoming supported elements.</p>\n<p>Local independent evidence: all three original n_number_plaque.cab resources match Windows Expand byte for byte (6,353 decoded bytes), checked by the new <code>cabinet-verify</code> example against <code>target/cabinet-reference-01/</code>. Six synthetic tests cover archive/XML truncation, checksum corruption, dictionary dependency, declared output bounds, reserve fields, unsafe paths, unsupported compression, entities, nesting and malformed documents. The 126 workspace test executions with all features pass. That stage did not yet draw the plaque: GaugeString, ATC-ID binding, fonts and daytime/nighttime text colors are the next work.</p>\n<h3>Previous completed instrument/reference stage</h3>\n<p>Commits <code>e2eceb5</code> and <code>09b44c5</code> completed the current opt-in C172 panel: five live air-data needles plus the native heading card/two attitude sprites. The current stage adds optional reference schema 2: 12 control readings and 32 instrument/clock/loading/state readings in one 648-byte SimConnect packet. Default schema-1 capture and pose import remain supported.</p>\n<p>The recorder&#39;s extended live request is not yet verified against a running original FSX. The build emits original synthetic fixtures; schema-2 C#/Rust interoperability is checked without game files in the Windows CI workflow. The sequential reader validates bounds, field set, source/title/object, contiguous indices and elapsed wall time. Absolute simulator time is kept separate and can pause/rewind.</p>\n<p><code>reference-gauge-replay</code> feeds recorded IAS, indicated altitude, VSI and gyro readings directly to the native C172 profile. Available stateless model bindings also use recorded controls/variables. It normalizes simulator time and explicitly restarts native state after rewinds, including rewinds from normalized time zero. Failed/caged/adjusted instruments or changed native inputs on a paused clock are outside the checked replay profile. ISA/body/gyro differences are diagnostic output only; aircraft trajectory and full instrument behavior are not replayed.</p>\n<p>Local evidence: <code>target/reference-protocol-check-03/</code> contains a C#-derived five-row synthetic sequence and malformed-case checks. Native replay under <code>target/reference-instrument-replay-sequence-02/</code> passed five rows, one paused reuse and one clock rewind with a new helper PID. The native VSI returned 600.0000192 for the recorded 600 fpm input. First/last fixed-camera images were rendered and local images inspected. Raw resources/recordings remain ignored.</p>\n<p>The existing live-flight gauge check remains available; instructions and known limits are in <a href=\"#/docs/VIEWER\">the viewer guide</a> and <a href=\"#/docs/FORMATS\">format notes</a>.</p>\n<p>Validation: 120 passing workspace test executions with all features; both C# schema self-tests and C#/Rust protocol regressions; release viewer/replay builds, release schema-2 pose import and five-row native replay. The existing 121-frame live-flight gauge check also passed pause/reset/view preservation unchanged. Architecture, formatting and generated documentation checks pass locally. The CI workflow is configured for the synthetic protocol gate; no remote CI run or extended real-FSX acquisition is claimed by this local checkpoint.</p>\n<p>One initial debug render exited with Windows STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE during GPU startup; subsequent debug integration and both release snapshots passed. The cause is not identified; investigate if it recurs rather than claiming that all GPU startup paths have been established.</p>\n<h2>Next acceptance work</h2>\n<ol><li>Establish the FSX text/default-font contract and validate pixels when reference</li></ol>\n<p>   acquisition resumes. Add actual time-of-day/panel-light state and authored    night-bitmap selection before calling this general day/night XML support.    Extend strings beyond the current single-line ASCII/conditional subset.</p>\n<ol><li>When the user resumes original-FSX acquisition, verify schema 2 against a</li></ol>\n<p>   loaded C172 flight and compare indications/cockpit images with exact    weather/loading/realism settings. Acquisition is currently deferred.</p>\n<ol><li>Add authored temporal response only after establishing the native lag contract.</li><li>Validate gyro filtering/placement and add drift, failures and adjustment</li></ol>\n<p>   controls; extend other moving faces. Model engine/propeller state before    treating tachometer input as telemetry.</p>\n<ol><li>Extend XML gauges, digital text, interaction and PANELS services as tested</li></ol>\n<p>   subsets. Do not present decoded static artwork as functional readings.</p>\n<ol><li>Continue reference flight validation, broader aircraft dynamics, airport/BGL</li></ol>\n<p>   scenery and menu parity. See <a href=\"#/docs/COMPATIBILITY\">compatibility</a> for current gaps.</p>\n<p>Keep this checkpoint current at each completed stage so interrupted work can resume from concrete evidence. Preserve unfinished changes until verified.</p>"
  },
  "CONTRIBUTING": {
    "source": "CONTRIBUTING.md",
    "title": "Contributing",
    "html": "<h1>Contributing</h1>\n<p>Start with architecture and compatibility. Keep claims tied to executable behavior and repeatable evidence.</p>\n<ul><li>Write source comments, documentation, issues and pull requests in English.</li><li>Never commit the FSX installation, SDK binaries, extracted geometry, textures or commercial add-ons.</li><li>Use small original fixtures. Test truncation, invalid references and resource limits as well as successful decoding.</li><li>Keep subsystem code in its <code>crates/fsx-*</code> package; the headless tool belongs in <code>tools/fsx-check</code>, launcher markup in <code>launcher/</code>, and shaders in <code>assets/shaders/</code>.</li><li>Run <code>cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked</code>, <code>cargo fmt --all --check</code>, <code>node scripts/check-architecture.mjs</code> and the fsx-app viewer build when changing rendering interfaces.</li><li>Regenerate the site with <code>node scripts/build-site.mjs</code> after editing <code>docs/</code>.</li><li>When visible engine behavior changes, refresh the website&#39;s selected screenshots with real openFSX captures and update their captions and capture record. Keep source game resources and intermediate captures excluded.</li><li>Describe verified formats and behavior; distinguish future goals from working features.</li></ul>\n<p>The owner has not selected a source license. Discuss licensing before submitting external implementation code.</p>"
  }
};
